export const GUIDE_TEXT = "# How the week works\nOperator keeps strength work minimal and sub-maximal so conditioning can carry the real weekly load. Three lifts, three sets, never near failure.\n\nMon: Operator Day 1: squat, bench, Lift 3 + accessories\nTue: HIC: MAP\nWed: Operator Day 2: squat, bench, Lift 3 + accessories\nThu: Plyos first, rest 10 min, then HIC: Anaerobic\nFri: Operator Day 3: squat, bench, deadlift + accessories\nSat: HIC: Threshold / Long HIC alternating, or LISS after a heavy week\nSun: Off\n\n## Strength rules\n\n- Sets can range 3–5 depending on what you can handle. Deadlift stays 1–3 sets.\n- Minimum 2 min rest between sets; 5 min on heavy squat and deadlift weeks.\n- If a set grinds at 70–80%, your max is too high. Lower it rather than pushing through.\n- Ramp: skip the 85% single on light weeks; add a 90% single on heavy weeks. Deadlift needs only 2–3 ramp sets; Lift 3 needs one light set of 10 and one at 70%.\n\n## Cluster lifts and their variants\n\nEach cluster lift has a list of variants, and the first on each list is the reference — the lift your stored max belongs to. Pick the one your block actually runs in Setup → Program; it is a per-cycle choice, like your accessories, because the wave builds to heavy weeks on a max for a particular lift and changing it mid-block makes those weeks mean nothing.\n\n- The block's lift uses the max you stored, unscaled. Choose the safety squat bar for a cycle and the number you type is your safety squat bar max. The app does not derive it from a back squat you were never asked for.\n\n- A one-day swap is scaled. Change the lift on a single day's card and the weight is your block max times the ratio between the two variants, so a front squat on a Wednesday is prescribed off the squat you are actually training.\n\n- Test and bridge weeks always use the reference, even against an explicit swap. Otherwise a front-squat single would be written back as a back-squat max and quietly drop every weight in the next cycle.\n\n- Variants change the working weight and nothing else. Personal records, the strength charts and the cycle review all stay on the reference lift.\n\n- Your own variants go in Setup: a name and its share of the reference max is the whole prescription. Anything from 25 to 150%.\n\nA variant is a barbell — a straight bar, or a specialty bar: safety squat, trap, football, axle. Dumbbell, kettlebell and landmine work is accessory work and lives in the accessory slots, which is where you will find it. The rule gives the same answer as the mechanical one and is easier to apply: if the limit is what your hands can hold, or there is no lockout to test, there is no honest one-rep max and the percentages would be invented. Hence no goblet squats, no double-kettlebell front squats and no Romanian deadlifts here — they are in the accessory and travel-week lists.\n\nThe weighted pull-up is the exception, and it is an exception to the wording rather than to the reason: change the grip and it is still the same lift with the same testable max, loaded off a belt. Its percentages work differently, though, because the max you store is the added weight while the variant is a share of what you actually lift. A chin-up at 105% means 105% of bodyweight plus the belt, so at 180 lb with +60 on it — 240 in all — the chin-up equivalent is 252, which is +72 rather than +63. The app does that arithmetic; it needs a recent bodyweight to do it, and says nothing rather than guessing if it has not got one.\n\nSquat\n\n| Variant | Of the reference max | What it is\n| High-bar back squat | reference | Bar on the traps, upright torso, deeper knee bend. The reference: everything else here is a share of it.\n| Low-bar back squat | 103% | Bar on the rear delts, more hip and more forward lean. Usually a few percent heavier than high bar.\n| Safety squat bar squat | 90% | Cambered bar, handles in front. The weight sits forward and wants to fold you, so the upper back works hard. Usually about 10% under a straight-bar squat, but the gap is personal.\n| Front squat | 85% | Rack position, vertical torso, quads and upper back. About 85% of a back squat, and the upper back usually gives out first.\n| Box squat | 95% | Sit to a box at or just below parallel, pause, drive up. Kills the stretch reflex, so it is honest hip strength.\n| Paused squat | 90% | Two seconds in the hole, no bounce. Exposes whether the bottom position is actually under control.\n| Zercher squat | 77% | Bar in the crooks of the elbows. Brutal on the upper back and trunk, and the limiter is usually how much your arms will take.\nBench press\n\n| Variant | Of the reference max | What it is\n| Bench press | reference | Straight bar, flat, feet down, touch and press. The reference: everything else here is a share of it.\n| Close-grip bench | 92% | Hands about shoulder width, elbows tucked. More triceps, less shoulder, and the usual answer to a front delt that complains on the wide grip.\n| Incline barbell bench | 82% | Thirty to forty-five degrees. Upper chest and front delt, which is the one gap three days of flat pressing leaves.\n| Floor press | 92% | Flat on the floor, upper arms stop on the way down. No leg drive and no stretch at the bottom, so it is lockout strength and it is kind to the shoulder.\n| Paused bench | 93% | A dead stop on the chest, no bounce. The chest gives you a reference to settle against and start from, which is why this is the one that carries over to a competition bench.\n| Spoto press | 90% | Stop an inch or two above the chest and hold it there — no contact at all. Nothing to settle against, in the worst leverage of the lift, so it is harder than a paused bench and it catches a bar that was quietly sinking into the chest.\n| Swiss bar bench | 92% | Multi-grip (football) bar, palms facing or angled in. The one to run for a whole block when the shoulder will not tolerate a straight bar. Usually a little under a straight-bar bench, and the handle you pick changes it.\n| Cambered Swiss bar bench | 88% | A Swiss bar with the handles dropped below the sleeves, so the hands travel past the chest. Extra range at the bottom and a longer press out of it: harder than the flat Swiss bar, and the gap is personal — give it its own max if yours differs.\n| Pin or board press | 105% | Short range from pins or boards, overloading the top half. Easy to flatter yourself with — pick one height and keep it.\nOverhead press\n\n| Variant | Of the reference max | What it is\n| Overhead press | reference | Standing, strict, no leg drive and no layback beyond a braced trunk. The reference: everything else here is a share of it.\n| Push press | 125% | A dip and drive off the legs, then press it out. More weight overhead and more total-body coordination; the lockout is still yours.\n| Seated barbell press | 95% | Takes the legs and the hips out of it. Less weight, and nowhere to hide a sloppy brace.\n| Behind-the-neck press | 90% | Wants genuine shoulder mobility and gives a narrow margin for error. Worth it for upper-back and overhead position if you have the range; skip it if you do not.\n| Z press | 72% | Seated on the floor, legs straight. Removes every bit of help from below the waist, which is why the number is so much lower.\n| Pin press | 105% | Dead start from pins at forehead height. No stretch reflex and no dip, so it is the sticking point on its own.\nWeighted pull-up\n\n| Variant | Of the reference max | What it is\n| Weighted pull-up | reference | Pronated, hands about shoulder width, chin over the bar. The reference: everything else here is a share of it.\n| Weighted chin-up | 105% | Supinated, hands shoulder width. More biceps and a shorter effective pull, so most people are strongest here.\n| Neutral-grip pull-up | 103% | Palms facing, on handles or a parallel bar. Usually the kindest on the elbow and the shoulder, and close to a chin-up for strength.\n| Wide-grip pull-up | 95% | Hands well outside the shoulders. Shorter range but a worse angle for the lats and arms, and it goes down rather than up.\n| Chest-to-bar pull-up | 92% | Pull until the chest touches. A longer range and real upper-back retraction at the top, which is where it gets expensive.\nDeadlift\n\n| Variant | Of the reference max | What it is\n| Conventional deadlift | reference | The reference. Everything else here is expressed against it.\n| Sumo deadlift | 100% | Wide stance, hands inside the knees, shorter bar path and more quad. Treated as equal to conventional because the gap is personal — if yours differs, give it its own max.\n| Trap bar deadlift | 105% | Neutral grip, load closer to the hips, easier on the lower back. Usually a touch heavier than conventional, more so from the high handles.\n| Deficit deadlift | 90% | Standing on 1–3 inches. Longer pull off the floor, harder start, and the reason to use it is a weak break from the floor.\n| Snatch-grip deadlift | 85% | Wide grip, much longer range, heavy on the upper back. Grip usually decides the set.\n| Paused deadlift | 85% | Pause an inch or two off the floor, or below the knee. Punishes any slack in the start position.\n| Block or rack pull | 110% | Bar raised to just below the knee. Shorter pull, heavier weight, and easy to overload — keep it honest.\n\n## Pull-ups\n\nThe one accessory the app programs rather than just lists, because it is the one that takes years and responds to being told what to do next. Wednesday's card prints the prescription for whichever rung your best logged set puts you on, and moves you up on its own.\n\n| Best set | Rung | The work | Moves you up\n| 0 | Hangs and negatives\nThe negative builds the top half and the grip at the same time, and it is the only thing that works when you cannot yet pull your own weight. | 5 × 10–20 s dead hang, then 4–6 negatives: jump or step to the top, lower for 5 seconds, no faster. | One clean rep from a dead hang.\n| 1+ | Band-assisted volume\nVolume is what moves this. A band you can barely finish with is doing too little; one that makes it easy is doing too much. | 4 × 4–6 with the lightest band that lets you finish the set clean. Keep the negatives. | Five clean reps in one set.\n| 5+ | Clean reps\nReps in reserve matter more here than grinding: the set that leaves you shaking costs you the next two sessions. | 4–5 sets across, stopping two short of failure. Full hang at the bottom, chin clearly over at the top. | Ten clean reps in one set.\n| 10+ | Ready to load\nTen clean reps is the usual threshold where adding weight beats adding reps. The app can run the weighted pull-up as Lift 3 from here. | Add weight: start at 10% of bodyweight for 4 × 3–5, and run it as Lift 3 on the Operator percentages. | A weighted single at 25% of bodyweight.\n| 15+ | Weighted\nFrom here it is ordinary strength work: the percentages apply to bodyweight plus the added weight. | Weighted pull-ups as a cluster lift, retested like any other max. | Keep adding, and keep one bodyweight set a week for the reps.\n\nWeighted pull-ups as Lift 3. Once you are on the last rung they can be a cluster lift like any other, and the percentages apply to bodyweight plus the added weight — so the app works out what to hang off the belt from your seven-day average bodyweight, not from the number on the plate. A rung is read from your history at the date you are looking at, so a day last spring shows the rung you were on then.\n\n## Conditioning: Black\n\n| Format | Session | System\n| MAP | 8–10 × 1 min hard / 1 min easy | Max aerobic power\n| Anaerobic | 6–8 × 30 sec near-max / 2 min easy | Glycolytic\n| Threshold | 4 × 4 min hard / 3 min easy | Threshold / VO2\n| Long HIC | 5 × 3 min hard / 90 sec easy | Aerobic power\n| LISS | 30–45 min conversational | Aerobic base\n| FOBBIT | An easy base, broken by bouts of work | Aerobic base with bursts\n| Endurance Predator | A long walk, broken by all-out sprints | Aerobic base with sprints\n| Anabolic Sprints | 30 m flat out × 5–10, walk back between | Alactic power\n| RESET-20 | 20 sec flat out / 2–5 min rest × 3–5 | Alactic / anaerobic\n| Hill Sprints | 10–20 sec up a steep hill × 3–10 | Alactic power\n| Freeform | Your own hard and easy times | Your call\nThese are the shapes. Tactical Barbell II’s forty named sessions, Meat Eater and Apex Hills among them, are below under Conditioning: the book’s HIC sessions, and in the same format list on the day.\n\n- Black sets the dose, not the tool. The echo bike is your default; switch activity on any HIC or LISS day, from the list below.\n- Keep the format and activity fixed to track progress. Results only compare within the same activity.\n- If most sessions are on a bike, watch hip flexors and saddle position: it compounds with squats and deadlifts.\n- Running and rucking add impact and back load. Plyos come first on whichever day they land, so keep sprint volume low that day.\n\n| Activity | Hard sessions measured in | Steady measured in\n| Echo bike | Total calories | Total calories\n| Rower | Total calories | Distance (m)\n| Ski erg | Total calories | Distance (m)\n| Stair machine | Floors | Floors\n| Run / sprints | Distance (yd) | Distance (mi)\n| Cycling | Average watts | Distance (mi)\n| Trail run · climbs | Distance (yd) | Distance (mi)\n| Ruck · climbs | Distance (mi) | Distance (mi)\n| Hike · climbs | Distance (mi) | Distance (mi)\n| Hill sprints · climbs | Distance (yd) | —\n| Swim | Distance (m) | Distance (m)\n| Other | — | —\n\nEach has its own warm-up and its own advice, printed on the day. Results only ever compare within the same activity and format, so switching tools does not corrupt your history — it just starts another thread of it.\n\nClimbing counts as distance. Trail runs, hikes and rucks ask for elevation gain, and the app turns it into flat-equivalent distance at 1000 ft per mile — so five miles with 3,000 feet of climbing reads as eight miles flat. The raw numbers stay on screen next to it. That equivalent is what “last” and “best” compare, so a brutal climbing day stops looking worse than an easy flat one, and Status keeps a climbing total for the week and the last four. Change the factor in Setup if a thousand feet is not your mile. Nothing else in the programming reads it: it is there to compare sessions, not to move your deloads around.\n\nBenchmarks. One activity-and-format pairing you keep coming back to, so there is something in the log that is genuinely comparable over a year. Pin one in Setup, or leave it and the app takes whichever hard pairing you have the most results for. Status charts it with your best and the days since, and on a conditioning day where it is due — every 35 days — the check-in asks for it. A format with no number to compare, like a FOBBIT, cannot be a benchmark.\n\nStuck in a rut? Six hard sessions in a row sharing a format or a tool and the app says so. Steady work is exempt: it is meant to be samey.\n\nOptional sets (Operator I/A). K. Black's intermediate/advanced take on Operator hands you the volume decision: rather than a fixed three sets, you work somewhere in a range and choose on the day. Tap + at the end of the sets on any lift card and you get another one, for that lift on that day only — the program is not touched and tomorrow is back to normal. If you want the room there every week instead, give the week an Up to value in Setup. Either way the surplus sets show dashed, and nothing is counted as missed if you skip them. In Ageless Athlete Jim Madden calls this the part of I/A he considers essential, and a few extra sets on weighted pull-ups his favorite way to add upper-body size without derailing recovery. Deadlift has always worked this way here: one set required, up to three.\n\nWhat this is not. Full Operator I/A also floats your lifting days 48 to 72 hours apart, so the wave advances by session rather than by week. This app runs on a calendar, so it does not do that — and Madden says he mostly keeps a fixed three-sessions-a-week schedule himself, taking his variability in sets and intensity instead. That is the part you have here.\n\nWhat matters today. Before a session you pick up to three things to pay attention to, and afterwards you get an account of them. Three is the cap on purpose: any more and none of them is a focus.\n\nThe questions depend on the day. Lifting days ask about execution — finishing every set, a proper warm-up, keeping technique tight, stopping short of failure. Conditioning days ask about where the effort went, which is the thing that actually goes wrong on a bike: hitting the rounds, holding the pace, keeping the easy parts easy, finishing able to do one more. A question that cannot apply is not asked — a steady session is never asked about rounds. Test days and Base Building circuits set no aims at all.\n\nSome the app can score from what you logged, and does. The rest only you can judge, so those ask you once at the end rather than being guessed at. The difference is visible: a scored line tells you what it counted, a self-rated line asks.\n\nThe note is the point. What you write in “anything to remember” is shown to you before your next session of the same kind — your last squat note before you squat again, not Tuesday's bike session. A note nobody reads again is just journalling.\n\nEasy week. Every third week the conditioning load comes down, and it is meant to land on the wave's 90% and 95% weeks so the heavy lifting gets the energy. The app does this for you: fewer rounds, shorter LISS, on weeks 3 and 6 of each cycle. It is not a week you have missed.\n\nFOBBITs. Named for the soldier who never leaves the forward operating base — the session you can run with no ground to cover and barely any kit. A FOBBIT is not one workout, it is a shape: hold an easy aerobic base, step off it every so often for a bout of work, get back on.\n\nBeyond that the sources disagree on nearly everything, so the app does not pretend otherwise. Tactical Barbell II gives the original — two minutes of base, then swings, then snatches. Mass Protocol gives a shorter one and says plainly that you are not restricted to kettlebells. The author then published three more as Mods: one that buys press volume at 70–75% of your 1RM every three to five minutes, one that rotates three strength-endurance movements, and one that runs the other way round, with a two to three minute striking drill off sixty to ninety seconds of base.\n\nThe rule that matters. In the author’s words: any FOBBIT over 30 minutes is an E, anything under is a HIC. So the length you pick is not cosmetic — it decides whether the session counts against your hard days or your easy ones, and the app counts it the way you logged it.\n\nA bout of reps is not on the clock: the timer holds at it until you tap Done. A timed bout — an AMRAP, a striking round — is, so it counts toward the session.\n\n| FOBBIT | Kit | Base | The rotation | Source\n| Kettlebell | One kettlebell | 2 min | Kettlebell swing × 20, then kettlebell snatch × 10 / arm | Tactical Barbell II\n| Mass Protocol | One kettlebell | 2 min | Kettlebell swing × 10 | Mass Protocol\n| Maximal strength | A press you can leave loaded | 3 min to 5 min | Overhead press × 3 at 70% to 75% | TB article, Mod #1\n| Strength-endurance | A pull-up bar and a floor | 2 min | Push-up × 10, then pull-up × 10, then sit-up × 10 | TB article, Mod #2\n| Strength-endurance, AMRAP | A pull-up bar and a floor | 2 min | Push-up × 60 s AMRAP, then pull-up × 60 s AMRAP, then sit-up × 60 s AMRAP | TB article, Mod #2\n| Combat | A bag, or room to shadow box | 60 s to 90 s | Heavy bag × 2 min, then straight punches × 2 min, then shadow boxing × 2 min | TB article, Mod #3\n| Sledgehammer | A sledgehammer and a tire | 2 min | Sledgehammer strike × 10 / side, then push-up × 15 | Ours\n| Barbell push press | A loaded barbell | 2 min | Push press × 10 | Ours\n| Sandbag | One sandbag | 2 min | Shoulder it, alternating sides × 10, then bear-hug squat × 15 | Ours\n| Sandbag, heavy | A bag you would not want to carry far | 2 min | Ground to shoulder × 6 / side, then front carry × 30 m | Ours\n| Sandbag over bar | A sandbag and a bar you can fix at a height | 2 min | Sandbag over the bar × 5, then push-up × 15 | Ours\n| Sandbag over the shoulder | One sandbag and room behind you | 2 min | Over-the-shoulder toss × 10 | Ours\n| Bodyweight | Nothing at all | 2 min | Burpee × 10, then push-up × 15 | Ours\n| Dumbbells | Two dumbbells | 2 min | Thruster × 10, then renegade row × 10 / side | Ours\n\nPick a default in Setup and swap it on any single session from that day’s card. Something else lets you type your own movements, separated by a slash. Where a row says Ours the shape is the book’s and the movements and counts are the app’s — the sources sanction substituting but do not prescribe these.\n\nGood for weather, for a hotel, for a day when the bike is taken, and for keeping impact off the legs the day before a heavy squat.\n\n## Conditioning: the book’s HIC sessions\n\nTactical Barbell II gives forty named High Intensity Conditioning sessions in Part II, and Black leaves the choice of which ones to you: two or three a week, on any day, and the book asks that you rotate them rather than settle on one. Every one of the forty is a format here. Pick it from the format list on a conditioning day, in the Tactical Barbell II groups under the app’s own shapes, or set a session of your own on any day. The day’s card prints the prescription, the easier and harder versions, and what the book says about it; the timer runs each round, on the clock where the book times it and held until you tap Done where it counts reps or distance.\n\nWhat gets scored. Where the book says “for time” the app asks for the time, prefilled from the timer’s wall clock, and ranks it with the fastest as the best; an AMRAP is scored by rounds, Beat Your Face by burpees. Everything else is logged by rounds and minutes, as a FOBBIT is. A book session compares against itself whatever you ran it on, so it is a sound benchmark. The easy week every third week halves the rounds, the way it does for every format.\n\nChoosing. The book’s own words: sessions 1 to 10 mix aerobic and anaerobic work and 11 to 24 lean anaerobic; do not make the general conditioning sessions your only HIC work; and power development should never be the bulk of it. The author’s own favorite cluster is 600 Meter Resets, Apex Hills and LSS running. If he could keep one conditioning exercise for life it would be hill sprints, and he recommends making them a staple after Block I.\n\nAerobic / anaerobic · #1 to #24\nSessions 1 to 10 are a mix of aerobic and anaerobic work: maximum-effort intervals, hills, burpees and sledgehammer work that build the heart, the mitochondria and lactate tolerance. Sessions 11 to 24 primarily develop the anaerobic systems, with repeated near-maximal efforts and rests that are over before you have recovered. The book says not to worry about the difference: you get a mix of both here.\n\n### #1 Connaught Range 10 to 1s (Aerobic / anaerobic)\n\n- 10 burpees, sprint 100 m\n- 9 burpees, sprint back\n- 8 burpees, sprint 100 m … down to 1 burpee\n- Shuttle-run style, as quickly as you can\n\nBasic: Start with 5 to 1 and work up to 10. And/or cut the run to 50 m.\nAdvanced: Sprint the full 100 m. And/or add 10 squats at each stop, so burpees and squats both count down.\n\nFind a football field or a track, anywhere with 100 m of running room. The session comes from a member of the RCMP’s federal Emergency Response Team, where 10-to-1 burpees in this fashion were a regular part of morning PT for candidates. Your goal is to complete it as quickly as possible, so log the time and chase it.\n\nNeeds: 100 m of running room. Scored by time; lower is better. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #2 Fast 5 Tempo Run (Aerobic power)\n\n- Run 5 km (3 miles)\n- 80 to 85% of maximum heart rate\n\nBasic: Start with half the distance if you need to, and work up to the full distance.\nAdvanced: Pick up the pace, and/or run 4 to 5 miles.\n\nFaster than your LSS work: fast enough that you cannot hold a conversation easily. Get a little outside your comfort zone. It is still one continuous effort, so it trains the aerobic engine at the top of its range rather than the anaerobic systems.\n\nNeeds: somewhere to run 5 km. Scored by time; lower is better. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #3 600 Meter Resets (Max aerobic power)\n\n- Sprint 600 m, maximum effort\n- Rest 3 to 5 minutes\n- × 6\n\nBasic: 4 reps.\nAdvanced: 10 reps.\n\nThe long rest is the point. The benefit comes from a maximum effort made from a rested state: you do yourself no favors sprinting the next rep while still exhausted, and that kind of training is elsewhere in the book for a different purpose. Think of it as strength training for the heart: it develops the strength of cardiac contraction and builds mitochondria. Jog 5 to 10 minutes first. Each 600 is all out, then rest until you are relatively well recovered.\n\nNeeds: a track, or 600 m of running room. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #4 Heavy Bag Resets (Max aerobic power)\n\n- Heavy bag strikes 90 s, maximum effort\n- Rest 2 to 5 minutes\n- × 5\n\nBasic: 4 rounds, and/or 60 s of striking instead of 90.\nAdvanced: Up to 10 rounds, and/or 2 minutes of striking.\n\nThe same principle as the resets: intense work springing from a well-rested state. Any striking you like, punches, kicks, knees, elbows, combos, as long as it is an all-out effort for the full 90 seconds. Your power will fade part way through; that is where the training happens, so keep pushing with whatever you have until the alarm. Light skipping or stretching between rounds is fine. A good one to tack on after a strength session when you are already in the gym, or run alone with a core or grip finisher.\n\nNeeds: a heavy bag. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #5 Indoor Power Intervals (Max aerobic power)\n\n- Treadmill run, Airdyne or row 2 minutes, max effort\n- Rest 3 to 5 minutes\n- × 5\n\nThe 600 m resets for people who cannot get to a track or are dealing with the weather. It is not an all-out sprint for two minutes but it should not be far behind: roughly 80 to 90% of max heart rate, a pace you can hold for the length of the interval. On a treadmill, something like 80% of your 100 m sprint speed, with the incline if the belt cannot go fast enough. Then slow to a walk and rest the full 3 to 5 minutes. Most training works you hard for 20 to 60 seconds; a sustained two-minute maximum takes your game somewhere else.\n\nNeeds: a treadmill, airdyne or rower. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #6 Sledge Drill (Anaerobic threshold)\n\n- Sledgehammer on tire 1 minute\n- Jump rope 1 minute\n- Sledgehammer 30 seconds\n- Jump rope 30 seconds\n- Rest 2 to 3 minutes\n- × 5\n\nBasic: 3 rounds.\nAdvanced: 1 minute for each of the four, 5 rounds.\nAlternative: Dumbbell or barbell push press in place of the sledge and tire.\n\nSwitch hands and grip now and then. You want powerful, consistent blows you can keep up, not an all-out frenzy: a sustained effort for the duration. It is an anaerobic-threshold session, built to lengthen how long you can work near your maximum, with strength-endurance, power-endurance and work capacity thrown in.\n\nNeeds: a sledgehammer, a tire and a jump rope. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #7 Black on Oxygen (Aerobic / anaerobic)\n\n- Kettlebell swings 60 seconds (or 30 swings)\n- Run 800 m\n- Rest 2 to 3 minutes\n- × 5\n\nBasic: 3 rounds.\nAdvanced: A heavier bell, or the same bell swung for 2 minutes instead of 1.\n\nLearned from a former US Army Ranger: the solo version of a partner drill where one swings while the other runs two laps, then they switch, continuously. Take the bell to the track: 16 to 32 kg for men with 24 about the mean, 8 to 24 kg for women. The aim is continuous higher-intensity work for several minutes. Swing at a steady, comfortable pace and switch hands halfway; if you have to set it down before the minute is up, rest briefly and carry on. Then drop it and run the 800 as quickly as you can. Stick to 2 to 3 minutes of rest; you do not have to be fully recovered today.\n\nNeeds: a kettlebell and a track. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #8 BOO II (Aerobic / anaerobic)\n\n- 10 burpees\n- Run 800 m\n- 5 burpees\n- Run 400 m, maximum effort\n- Rest 2 to 3 minutes\n- × 3\n\nBasic: 5 burpees, 400 m, 5 burpees, 400 m max effort, rest 2 to 3 min, × 2.\nAdvanced: 20 burpees, 800 m, 10 burpees, 400 m max effort, rest 2 min, × 3 to 5.\n\nEach round as quickly as you can. The 800 is not a sprint but it is faster than a jog; drop straight into the burpees, then give the final 400 everything. You do not have to be fully rested between rounds, so try not to run over the rest.\n\nNeeds: a track. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #9 FOBBIT Intervals (20 min)\n\nThe book’s ninth session is the FOBBIT: two minutes of easy base, twenty swings, two minutes, ten snatches an arm, until the treadmill reads twenty minutes. It has its own section above, with the Mass Protocol and Mod variations, and its own picker on the day.\n\n### #10 Short Hills (Aerobic / anaerobic)\n\n- Hill sprint, 10 to 15 seconds up\n- Walk back down as the rest (30 to 60 s)\n- × 10\n\nBasic: × 5, work up to 10.\nAdvanced: × 15 to 20, and/or a 10 to 20 lb weight vest.\n\nFor this one you want a shorter hill, roughly 10 to 15 seconds to sprint up, nothing longer or higher. The walk down is the rest; at the bottom, go again. If the author could keep one conditioning exercise for life it would be hill sprints: nothing builds legs, heart, lungs and willpower like them, and after Block I he recommends making them a staple.\n\nNeeds: a hill of 10 to 15 seconds. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #11 Oxygen Debt 101 (Glycolytic)\n\n- Sprint 200 m, maximum effort, × 3 with 30 seconds between\n- Rest 3 minutes\n- × 3 to 4\n\nBasic: 2 rounds, work up to 4 over time.\nAdvanced: All 4 rounds.\n\nFrom a nationally ranked runner and former police academy instructor. It is built to work the lactic system and raise your tolerance for pain: three maximum 200s with only 30 seconds between them make one round, and the three minutes between rounds is not enough to clear it.\n\nNeeds: a track. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #12 Speed-Endurance Ladders (Speed-endurance)\n\n- 400 m, rest 40 s\n- 300 m, rest 30 s\n- 200 m, rest 20 s\n- 100 m, rest 10 s\n- Then back up: 100 m, rest 20 s, 200 m, rest 30 s, 300 m, rest 40 s, 400 m\n- All running at maximum effort\n\nBasic: Down the ladder only. Rest a full 2 minutes at the bottom before coming back up.\nAdvanced: Down and back up is one round. Keep going for as many rounds as you can, with a 2-minute rest at the bottom between some rounds if you need it.\n\nEvery rung is a maximum effort and the rests shrink as the distances do, which is what makes it speed-endurance rather than speed. Work down from the 400, rest ten seconds after the 100, and climb straight back.\n\nNeeds: a track. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #13 Meat Eater (Aerobic / anaerobic)\n\n- Sprint 100 m\n- 20 Russian kettlebell swings (24 to 48 kg)\n- Walk back to the start\n- × 10\n\nBasic: × 5, with a lighter bell if you need one.\nAdvanced: For time, as quickly as possible: jog back instead of walking. And/or a heavier bell.\nAlternative: A dumbbell for the swings.\n\nUse a moderate to medium-heavy bell and leave it on the track 100 m away, near the finish line. Sprint flat out to it, pick it up and swing, then turn around and walk back. Two-handed swings are the author’s preference, but one-handed works. The time is only scored in the advanced version; the standard one is paced by the walk.\n\nNeeds: a kettlebell and 100 m of track. Scored by time; lower is better. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #14 Meat Eater II (Glycolytic)\n\n- 10 Russian kettlebell swings\n- 10 burpees\n- Rest 60 seconds\n- × 10, for time\n\nBasic: × 5, with a lighter bell if you need one.\nAdvanced: A heavier bell, one you can barely swing for the full 10. Leave a swing or two in the bank to keep your form.\n\nDeceptively simple, highly effective. A medium to heavy bell for you, two-handed Russian swings. If you only have light bells, swing one-handed and do 10 per arm. It does not matter whether you start with the burpees or the swings. The clock runs through the rests, so the whole thing is one time.\n\nNeeds: one kettlebell. Scored by time; lower is better. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #15 Disarmed (Aerobic / anaerobic)\n\n- 10 burpees\n- Heavy bag strikes 2 minutes\n- Rest 60 seconds\n- × 3 to 5\n- Finisher: Plank & Shank, 3 minutes × 3\n\nBasic: Bag 1 minute instead of 2.\nAdvanced: Bag 3 minutes.\n\nWork hard on the bag: steady strikes, with combos approaching maximum effort and speed. After the rounds, rest a minute or two and move to the finisher: 30 seconds of plank, 30 seconds of shank, until three minutes is up, with 30 to 60 seconds of rest after each pair if you need it, three times over. The shank is a static back extension held at the top.\n\nNeeds: a heavy bag. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #16 Standard Issue Hills (Aerobic / anaerobic)\n\n- Hill sprint, maximum effort\n- Walk or jog down\n- Rest 1 to 2 minutes at the bottom\n- × 5 to 10\n\nBasic: 3 to 4 rounds, working up to 5 to 10 over time. Stretch the rests to 3 minutes.\nAdvanced: Jog down and take no rest at the bottom.\n\nThe basic hill session, and do not let its simplicity fool you: it will probably do more for your conditioning than a complicated session with ten exercises and twenty toys. Aim for a hill that takes 30 to 45 seconds to sprint up; if all you have is a short one of 10 to 15 seconds, double the reps. Maximum effort and speed up, the rest starts at the bottom. Hills are to conditioning what barbells are to strength.\n\nNeeds: a hill of 30 to 45 seconds. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #17 Apex Hills (Aerobic / anaerobic)\n\n- Hill sprint\n- 10 Russian kettlebell swings at the top\n- Walk down\n- Rest 1 to 2 minutes\n- × 5 to 10\n\nBasic: 3 to 4 rounds, working up to 10. And/or a lighter bell.\nAdvanced: Jog down and drop the rest: the jog is the recovery. And/or a heavier bell, or double kettlebell swings. Operators work up to the 48 kg Beast for 15 or more rounds.\n\nOne of the simplest and most effective sessions in the book, the author’s personal favorite and a staple of his own conditioning. Simple, scalable, flexible, and it will turn you into a machine. A heavy bell with two-handed swings adds a power and strength element. Set the bell at the top, sprint to it flat out, bang out ten crisp swings, walk down; the rest starts at the bottom. No hill you can drive to? Hike out to one with the bell in a pack, and make sure the pack is comfortable, because you will be tired on the way back.\n\nNeeds: a kettlebell and a hill. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #18 Bloody Lungs (Aerobic / anaerobic)\n\n- 10 plank push-ups at the bottom\n- Hill sprint\n- 10 burpees at the top\n- Walk down\n- × 5\n\nBasic: An extra 1 to 2 minutes of rest at the bottom after the walk down. And/or 5 plank push-ups and burpees instead of 10.\nAdvanced: × 10, and/or jog down instead of walking. Operators: a 10 to 20 lb weight vest.\n\nThe plank push-up: start in the plank, push up onto one hand and then the other, do one full push-up from there, then go back down to the elbows one at a time. That is one rep. Ten of those, then sprint the hill with maximum effort, ten burpees at the top, and the walk down is your rest.\n\nNeeds: a hill. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #19 Bloody Lungs II (Aerobic / anaerobic)\n\n- 10 burpees\n- Hill sprint\n- 5 kettlebell or dumbbell snatches per arm at the top\n- Walk down\n- Rest 1 to 2 minutes\n- × 5 to 10\n\nBasic: × 5, working up to 10. A lighter bell to start. And/or 10 swings instead of snatches, and/or 5 burpees.\nAdvanced: × 10 or more. And/or 10 snatches per arm, and/or jog down, and/or no rest between rounds. Operators: a 10 to 15 lb vest.\nAlternative: Push presses or swings in place of the snatches.\n\nThe harder Bloody Lungs: the bell waits at the top. Burpees at the bottom, sprint straight up, snatch, walk down and rest.\n\nNeeds: a kettlebell or dumbbell and a hill. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #20 Anaerobic Capacity (Glycolytic)\n\n- Jog 800 m, sprint 400 m\n- Jog 800 m, sprint 400 m\n- Jog 400 m, sprint 200 m\n- Jog 400 m, sprint 200 m\n- Finisher: 50 plank push-ups\n\nBasic: 25 plank push-ups.\nAdvanced: A 5 to 10 lb weight vest.\n\nBest done at a track. The sprints are roughly 90 to 95% of maximum, and the whole sequence runs continuously with no rest: the jogs are the recovery. Then the finisher.\n\nNeeds: a track. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #21 Pepper Potting (Aerobic / anaerobic, under load)\n\n- Rucksack, pack or weight vest, 30 to 50 lb\n- A 1.5 mile route, or a 30-minute hike\n- Pepper-pot 100 m every 5 minutes\n\nA specialist drill, for people preparing for bootcamp or infantry training: it is patterned on fire and movement, and the author would not recommend it unless you are starting a military contract. It also happens to build aerobic and anaerobic capacity, strength-endurance and load bearing. Set a timer for every 5 minutes. When it goes, break into a run, and every fourth or fifth footfall drop to one knee, knee touching the ground, for two seconds, then up as fast as you can for another short bound. “Up, he sees me, down.” Keep the bounds short: as people tire they get longer, or the kneel turns into a rest, and that is exactly when to cut them short. Across the 100 m as fast as humanly possible, then the 5 minutes restart. The longer the route takes, the more pepper-potting you do.\n\nNeeds: a 30 to 50 lb ruck or vest and a route. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #22 Buffalo Laps (Aerobic / anaerobic)\n\n- 10 burpees\n- Run 400 m\n- 10 two-handed kettlebell swings (20 if one-handed)\n- Rest 45 to 60 seconds\n- × 4, for time\n\nBasic: Rest 60 seconds between rounds.\nAdvanced: Double kettlebell swings.\nAlternative: No kettlebell: one-handed dumbbell swings, 5 per side.\n\nSubmitted by a member of the RCMP who used it to bring his PARE time down from 3:15 to 2:54. The PARE is the force’s fitness test: a circuit of obstacles and stairs ending in a push-pull machine, built to simulate chasing, wrestling with and arresting a suspect, and anything in the two-minute range is the top percentile. The book’s header gives 45 to 60 seconds of rest and its prose says four rounds as fast as possible with none; the timer here follows the header, and you can skip the rest segment for the harder version.\n\nNeeds: a kettlebell and a track. Scored by time; lower is better. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #23 Meat Eater III (Strength, power and anaerobic)\n\n- 10 double kettlebell clean and press\n- Sprint 300 m\n- Lunge 100 m back to the start\n- × 4 to 6\n\nBasic: 5 clean and presses instead of 10. Skip the lunges and walk the 100 m back as the rest.\nAdvanced: Heavier bells, and/or 6 rounds or more.\nAlternative: One bell: 10 swings per arm, then 10 presses per arm.\n\nThis one is about strength, power and the anaerobic system. Two bells of equal weight at the start line, moderate to begin with, roughly 16 to 32 kg. Press, drop them, sprint 300 m, and if you are on a track lunge the last 100 m of the lap back to your bells.\n\nNeeds: two kettlebells and a track. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #24 Devil’s Trinity (Combat conditioning)\n\n- Kettlebell swings 1 minute\n- Burpees 1 minute\n- Heavy bag, sparring or shadow boxing 1 minute\n- Rest 1 minute\n- × 5\n\nBasic: 2 to 3 rounds.\nAdvanced: All 5 rounds without the rest. And/or double kettlebell swings.\nAlternative: With a partner, light sparring in place of the bag.\n\nDeceptively simple and very challenging, from a British paratrooper; the partner version had the two of them spar instead of hitting the bag. Set one-minute rounds. As many swings as you can in the minute, 12 to 24 kg for men and 8 to 16 kg for women, straight into burpees, then maximum effort on the bag with punches, knees, kicks and elbows.\n\nNeeds: a kettlebell and a heavy bag. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\nGeneral conditioning · #25 to #36\nWork capacity and general physical fitness: the ability to do more in less time, and to recover from it, which is what everything else in your training rests on. Most are done for time and bring several energy systems and strength qualities together in one session. Mix them with sessions 1 to 24; do not make them your only HIC work.\n\n### #25 GC 1: Beat Your Face (Work capacity)\n\n- Burpees 3 minutes, rest 3 minutes\n- Burpees 2 minutes, rest 2 minutes\n- Burpees 1 minute, rest 1 minute\n- × 1 to 3\n\nBasic: One round, and work up over time.\nAdvanced: Burpees 5 minutes, rest 2 minutes, × 3 to 4. And/or a 5 to 15 lb weight vest.\n\nInstead of push-ups, you beat your face with burpees. How many you can do in a set time is a great indicator of general conditioning, so do as many as you can in each block, record the total, and compare it over time. That total is what the app scores.\n\nNeeds: a floor. Scored by total burpees. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #26 GC 2 (Work capacity)\n\n- 10 pull-ups, 10 burpees, 10 squat jumps, 10 plyometric push-ups\n- Then 9 of each, 8 of each … down to 1\n- For time\n\nA descending ladder: finish the round of ten, then do the whole set again with nine, and keep going until you reach one. The pull-ups are what make it long, so scale them to what you have.\n\nNeeds: a pull-up bar. Scored by time; lower is better. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #27 GC 3: Brig Rat (Work capacity)\n\n- Burpees 30 s, dips 30 s\n- Burpees 30 s, squats 30 s\n- Burpees 30 s, back extensions 30 s\n- Rest 1 minute\n- × 3 to 5\n\nAdvanced: A 10 to 15 lb weight vest.\nAlternative: A plank in place of the back extensions, every round or every other round.\n\nThe squats are bodyweight. Three minutes of work on the clock, every other block burpees, then a minute to breathe.\n\nNeeds: a dip station. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #28 GC 4 (Work capacity)\n\n- 100 pull-ups, run 400 m\n- 100 push-ups, run 400 m\n- 100 kettlebell or dumbbell swings, run 400 m\n- 1 round, for time\n\nBasic: 50 reps of each exercise.\nAdvanced: A heavy bell, 48 kg or more, or double bells. And/or 2 rounds.\n\nIt can be done indoors with a treadmill or rower for the 400s, or step out of the gym, run roughly 400 m and come back in for the next exercise. Break the hundreds into whatever sets you need; the clock is the score.\n\nNeeds: a pull-up bar, a kettlebell and 400 m. Scored by time; lower is better. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #29 GC 5 (Work capacity)\n\n- A: max dips 1 minute, rest 90 s, max push-ups 1 minute, rest 90 s, × 3\n- Rest 2 minutes\n- B: 5 pull-ups, 10 burpees, × 3\n\nBasic: Dips and push-ups for 30 seconds instead of a minute.\nAdvanced: A weight vest for the dips, push-ups, pull-ups and/or burpees.\n\nDips at a station or on rings. Complete all of A, rest two minutes, then B.\n\nNeeds: a dip station and a pull-up bar. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #30 GC 6 (Work capacity)\n\n- 10 sledgehammer strikes on the tire\n- 5 burpees\n- 10 squats\n- As many rounds as possible in 5 minutes\n- Rest 60 to 90 seconds\n- × 3\n\nAdvanced: 10 burpees instead of 5. 5 rounds or more. And/or a 5 to 15 lb weight vest.\nAlternative: Kettlebell or dumbbell swings in place of the sledge.\n\nCycle through the three movements as many times as you can in five minutes, rest, and repeat for three rounds. Log the rounds you got through across all three blocks.\n\nNeeds: a sledgehammer and a tire. Scored by rounds completed. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #31 GC 7 (Work capacity)\n\n- 50 burpees\n- 50 squats\n- 50 diamond push-ups\n- Run 800 m\n- × 3, for time\n\nBasic: 1 to 2 rounds.\nAdvanced: 100 reps of each exercise, × 3 to 5.\n\nThree big rounds of bodyweight work with a run on the end of each. The book gives nothing but the numbers; the time is the whole story.\n\nNeeds: 800 m of running room. Scored by time; lower is better. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #32 GC 8 (Work capacity)\n\n- A: 10 kettlebell or dumbbell snatches per arm, 25 box jumps, 25 hanging knees to elbows, 25 dips, 5 burpees\n- 4 rounds, for time\n- B: handstand hold 60 seconds, rest 2 to 3 minutes, × 3\n\nBasic: 2 rounds of A, 1 round of B.\n\nAll of A before moving on to B. You can support yourself against a wall for the handstand holds. The time is for A.\n\nNeeds: a kettlebell, a box, a bar and dip station. Scored by time; lower is better. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #33 GC 9 (Work capacity)\n\n- A: 3 pull-ups, 5 burpees, 10 squats, as many rounds as possible in 10 minutes\n- B: 10 × 100 m sprints\n\nBasic: 5 × 100 m sprints instead of 10.\nAdvanced: Muscle-ups instead of pull-ups.\n\nAll of A before B, and aim for continuous work in A. The rounds you complete in the ten minutes are the score.\n\nNeeds: a pull-up bar and 100 m. Scored by rounds completed. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #34 GC 10 (Work capacity)\n\n- A: jog 2 minutes, 25 burpees, × 4\n- B: a core or grip finisher of your choice\n\nBasic: 15 burpees.\nAdvanced: 50 burpees. And/or a 5 to 20 lb weight vest.\n\nIndoors, a treadmill or rowing machine does the jogging. Treat the jog as active recovery and do the burpees as quickly as possible.\n\nNeeds: somewhere to jog. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #35 GC 11: Outside the Wire (Work capacity)\n\n- A: sprint 100 m, bear crawl 50 m, × 5, for time\n- B: 100 sledgehammer strikes on the tire, for time\n\nAdvanced: A 5 to 20 lb weight vest.\nAlternative: 10 muscle-ups for time, or 50 pull-ups for time, in place of the sledge.\n\nGet through A and B as quickly as you can. In A there is no rest between the sprint and the crawl: transition immediately.\n\nNeeds: 100 m, a sledgehammer and a tire. Scored by time; lower is better. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #36 GC 12 (Work capacity)\n\n- A: run 1.5 miles, for time\n- B: 20 barbell push presses, 20 back extensions, 20 pull-ups\n- × 3, for time\n\nBasic: 1 to 2 rounds of B.\n\nLight on the push press, roughly 15 to 30% of your estimated one-rep max; no need to calculate, guess and err on the side of lighter. Two clocks: the run, and the circuit.\n\nNeeds: a barbell, a back extension and a bar. Scored by time; lower is better. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\nPower development · #37 to #40\nPower, power-endurance and explosiveness. These should not make up the bulk of your HIC training: barring a specific goal, choose most of your sessions from 1 to 36 and sprinkle these in occasionally.\n\n### #37 BW Plyometric Power (Power)\n\n- A: 10 explosive plyometric push-ups, rest 90 s\n- 10 explosive jump squats, rest 90 s\n- 5 to 10 plyometric pull-ups, rest 2 minutes\n- × 3\n- B: 5 × 50 m sprints\n\nBasic: 2 rounds. And/or 5 plyometric pull-ups.\n\nEvery rep explosive and crisp. Power sessions should not make up the bulk of your HIC work: pick most of it from sessions 1 to 36 and sprinkle these in.\n\nNeeds: a pull-up bar and 50 m. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #38 Power Complex (Power)\n\n- Barbell push press × 5 at 60 to 70% of 1RM, rest 1 to 2 minutes\n- 10 double kettlebell or dumbbell squat jumps, 8 to 24 kg, rest 1 to 2 minutes\n- Kettlebell or dumbbell snatch × 5 per arm, 8 to 24 kg, rest 1 to 2 minutes\n- 5 plyometric pull-ups, rest 1 to 2 minutes\n- × 3\n\nBasic: 2 rounds, working up to 3. The lighter end of the weights. 3 plyo pull-ups instead of 5, and bodyweight jump squats if you like.\n\nEverything with maximum speed and explosiveness, but under control with good form at all times. The push press is fast on the way up and slower, controlled, on the way down; 60 to 70% is the weight you can still move with speed. Same principle for the squat jumps, onto a box if it helps. For the pull-ups, pull as forcefully and quickly as you can to the bar, then let go and drop: no need to lower yourself under control, this is power, not maximum strength.\n\nNeeds: a barbell, two kettlebells and a bar. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #39 Kinetic Conditioning (Power and speed)\n\n- Sprint 50 m, then 5 explosive plyometric push-ups\n- Rest 2 minutes\n- Sprint 50 m, then 5 explosive squat jumps\n- Rest 2 minutes\n- × 3\n\nBasic: 2 rounds.\n\nBuilt to increase power, speed and explosiveness, and the trick is to do every exercise at maximum intensity: as explosively, powerfully and quickly as you can. The two-minute rest is deliberate, so that you are fully rested before each maximum effort; you only get the benefit by going all out every rep. Take more than two minutes if you need it. Err on the side of more rest.\n\nNeeds: 50 m of running room. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### #40 Transition Complex (Strength to power)\n\n- Front squat × 3 at 85% of 1RM, then 10 squat jumps, rest 2 minutes\n- Standing overhead press × 3 at 85%, then 10 plyometric push-ups, rest 2 minutes\n- Weighted pull-ups × 3 (+10 to 50 lb), then 10 medicine ball slams\n- × 2 to 3\n\nBasic: Bodyweight pull-ups instead of weighted.\nAdvanced: Muscle-ups in place of the pull-ups. Plyo pull-ups in place of the slams.\nAlternative: 10 plyometric or kipping pull-ups instead of the medicine ball slams.\n\nHeavy standard lifting primes the system for power work. Finish the set of front squats, rack the bar at once, step back and do ten powerful, explosive bodyweight squat jumps. Rest a couple of minutes and move on. Estimate the one-rep maxes if you need to, there is no need to be exact; just make sure the front squat, the press and the pull-ups are heavy.\n\nNeeds: a rack, a barbell, a belt and a medicine ball. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\nNumbers, versions and reasoning are the book’s. Where a rest is given as a range the timer takes the low end, and the length it predicts for a bout of reps is the app’s estimate, used only to size the session before you start.\n\n## Conditioning: core, grip and the challenge sessions\n\nTactical Barbell II closes its training vault with two short lists. Core + Grip is four sessions: heavy strength work gives you some of both, the book says, “but every once in a while add in one of these finishers to take your abilities higher”. Three of them are finishers, tacked on after a main session, so here they are added to any day rather than picked instead of one: Today → Anything else today → Add a finisher. The fourth, Full Core, is a whole session and sits in the format list on a conditioning day.\n\nFinishers\n\nNothing is scored. The card remembers what you did last time, and for the grip circuit it applies the book’s rule: all the way round without putting the dumbbells down means 5 to 10 lb more a dumbbell next time. The hold buttons start the app’s ordinary timer.\n\n### Plank & Shank (1 to 3 rounds)\n\n- Plank 1 to 5 minutes\n- Shank 1 to 5 minutes\n- Rest 1 to 2 minutes\n- × 1 to 3\n\nBasic: Shorter holds to start, roughly 30 to 60 seconds each, and work up.\nAdvanced: Hold a weight when shanking, or wear a weight vest for both. Holds of 2 minutes or more. And/or drop the rest and keep moving from one hold to the next.\n\nA quick all-in-one core session and a good finisher. The shank is a static back extension: hold the top position of a back extension, spine fairly straight, no reps up and down, like a reverse plank for the back. It is harder than it sounds even for a strong deadlifter, and a good way to strengthen weak links in the posterior chain. Plank then shank with a break between, or superset them, or hold one longer than the other.\n\nNeeds: a back extension bench.\n\n### Quarterdeck Core (5 to 10 rounds)\n\n- Plank push-ups × 10\n- Back extensions × 10\n- V-ups × 10\n- Ab wheel or rollout × 5 to 10\n- × 5 to 10, resting as necessary\n\nBasic: 5 sets.\nAdvanced: A weighted vest, 10 to 20 lb.\n\nA core finisher with the weight on the abdominal muscles. Squats, deadlifts and overhead presses work the core, but mostly the rear of it, and less of the abs than you think, so every so often it is worth some direct work. The posterior chain is kept light here because the rest of your training already covers it, which makes this a good one to add after a posterior-chain-heavy session. The rollouts can be done with a barbell.\n\nNeeds: a back extension and an ab wheel or a barbell.\n\n### Progressive Grip Circuit (1 to 3 laps)\n\n- Farmer’s walk 100 m, then 10 dumbbell overhead presses\n- Farmer’s walk 100 m, then 10 dumbbell reverse curls\n- Farmer’s walk 100 m, then 10 dumbbell shrugs\n- Farmer’s walk 100 m, then 10 dumbbell hammer curls\n- Rest 2 to 3 minutes\n- × 1 to 3\n\nAlternative: In a gym: hold the dumbbells at your sides for 90 seconds in place of each 100 m walk.\n\nThe same pair of dumbbells for the whole session, both arms at once, and the goal is to get all the way round without putting them down. Start conservatively: the book suggests 20 to 50 lb a dumbbell for men and 5 to 30 lb for women. When you can get all the way round without putting them down, go up 5 to 10 lb a dumbbell. When you cannot, break it into its four segments with a brief rest after each, and work up to two in a row, then three, then all four. There are no basic or advanced versions of this one.\n\nNeeds: one pair of dumbbells and 100 m, or a gym.\n\nCore session\n\n### Full Core (Core)\n\n- A: Romanian deadlifts × 100, at 20 to 30% of 1RM\n- Rest 3 to 5 minutes\n- Hanging knees to elbows, or hanging leg raises, × 100\n- B, the finisher: Plank & Shank × 3 (60 s plank, 45 s shank)\n\nBasic: 50 deadlifts and 50 leg raises instead of 100.\nAdvanced: A weight vest for the plank and shank. And/or hold a weight when shanking.\n\nNot a finisher: a whole session on its own. Simple, minimalist and highly effective. Do the deadlifts and the leg raises in sets of 3, 5, 10, 20 or more, and rest as you need to. The author would rather pick the fewest highly effective movements and hit them hard than do ten kinds of sit-up. The book says 20 to 30% of 1RM without saying which lift; the app does not hold a Romanian deadlift max, so this is left for you to set, and lighter is the safer guess.\n\nNeeds: a barbell, a pull-up bar and a back extension. Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\nChallenge sessions\n\nFive, and the book is blunt about them: very difficult, optional, “not meant to be a part of your regular conditioning protocol” and not very healthy in a conventional sense. They are there to test your edges. Not until your conditioning is above average, once every couple of months, when fully rested, and get cleared by your physician first. So the app never schedules one and never makes one the benchmark; they are in the format list for the day you decide to.\n\n### Duku-Duku (Pretorian Hills) (Challenge)\n\n- Kettlebell goblet squats × 5 (24 kg for men, 12 kg for women)\n- Run up the hill, carrying the kettlebell\n- Kettlebell swings × 10 at the top\n- Walk down, still carrying it\n- × 5\n- The kettlebell cannot touch the ground for the whole session\n\nBasic: A light kettlebell. Put it down and rest 2 to 3 minutes after every round, at the bottom of the hill.\nAdvanced: 10 rounds. 10 goblet squats instead of 5. A 32 kg bell, working up to the 48 kg.\n\nLoosely based on a South African military selection exercise, where a candidate who put his load down was done. Carry the bell any way you like as long as it stays off the ground; a dumbbell works, a backpack does not. Even in great shape you may not finish all five, so work up: two rounds without putting it down, a break, then three. Pick a hill that takes 35 to 45 seconds to sprint up empty-handed, or a longer one run slower; with a short hill, do more rounds.\n\nNeeds: a hill and a 24 kg kettlebell (12 kg for women). Not scored by the book: the app logs rounds and minutes. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### Burpees for the Mentally Disturbed (Challenge)\n\n- Burpees for 10 minutes, as many as possible\n- Record the number\n- Rest 3 minutes\n- Burpees for 10 minutes, as many as possible: more than the first set\n\nBasic: 5 minute rounds.\nAdvanced: A weight vest, 5 to 15 lb.\n\nOn the second set, try to do more than you did on the first, and do not cheat the first by pacing yourself. The book asks only that the second beat the first; the app logs the two added together so there is one number to compare next time, and that total is the app’s choice.\n\nNeeds: a floor. Scored by total burpees. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### Triska-Deka-Phobia (Challenge)\n\n- Ruck march 13 miles, 50 lb load\n- Run 13 miles\n- For time\n\nBasic: 6 miles of each.\nAdvanced: An 80 to 100 lb ruck.\n\nUnlucky thirteen. Drop the ruck for the run. One clock for the whole of it.\n\nNeeds: a ruck with 50 lb. Scored by time; lower is better. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### Beasting (Challenge)\n\n- Carry a 48 kg kettlebell, or the same in a dumbbell, in your ruck\n- March or hike 3 miles\n- Every 10 minutes: 100 Russian swings, or 100 weighted squats, alternating\n- For time\n\nBasic: A 24 or 32 kg kettlebell, and 2 miles.\nAdvanced: Both the swings and the squats at every stop. And/or 6 miles instead of 3.\n\nPad the ruck extremely well. Drop it for the swings; leave it on, bell inside, for the squats. The ten-minute timer stops while you do the kettlebell work and starts again when you ruck up and walk, which is how the timer here runs it. The longer you take, the more swings and squats you owe.\n\nNeeds: a ruck and a 48 kg kettlebell. Scored by time; lower is better. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\n### Snake-Eater’s Delight (Challenge)\n\n- Day 1: ruck march 10 km with 50 lb, then run 10 km, for time\n- That night: stay awake. No sleep, no catnaps\n- Day 2, any time after 5 AM: the same ruck and run, and match or beat day 1\n\nBasic: Sleep 2 to 3 hours before day 2: bed at 2 AM, up at 5.\nAdvanced: Two nights without sleep between the two.\n\nA taste of what selection adds to physical work: sleep deprivation is the great equalizer, and how someone performs at their breaking point is what selection is looking for. The book is plain that there is no benefit to this beyond testing your limits, and that going without sleep is unhealthy. Pick it on each of the two days and log that day’s time.\n\nNeeds: a ruck with 50 lb, and two days. Scored by time; lower is better. The timer walks you through it: timed parts on the clock, bouts of reps or distance held until you tap Done.\n\nPrescriptions, versions and reasoning are the book’s. Scoring Burpees for the Mentally Disturbed by the two sets added together is the app’s choice, and the timer’s length for a bout of reps or distance is the app’s estimate.\n\n## Plyometrics\nA nervous-system stimulus, not a workout. Quality of each rep matters far more than how many you do: 15 to 20 minutes of actual work, once a week and never after conditioning. Phases rotate every 3 training weeks. Deload weeks use the extensive session at half volume.\n\nThey are not tied to a session. Thursday is the default, because that is where the week's middle conditioning day sits, but plyos are their own thing and move on their own. Open any day in a training week, tap Move…, and pick a day under Plyometrics. That changes the week you are looking at and nothing else; picking Thursday again clears it. Whatever was already on the day you choose stays — plyos go first, and on a conditioning day you get ten minutes between the two.\n\n| Phase | Exercise | Sets × reps | Contacts | Rest\n| Extensive\n~60 | Broad jump, single effort | 5 × 3 | 15 | 60 s\n| | Box jump, step down | 5 × 3 | 15 | 60 s\n| | Lateral bound, stick 2 sec | 4 × 4 / side | 16 | 60 s\n| | Pogo hops in place | 4 × 8 | 32 | 45 s\n| Unilateral + reactive\n~80 | Broad jump, 2 continuous | 5 × 2 jumps | 20 | 90 s\n| | Single-leg hop, straight line | 4 × 4 / side | 32 | 90 s\n| | Bound for distance | 4 × 6 contacts | 24 | 90 s\n| | Hurdle hops, low, continuous | 3 × 5 | 15 | 90 s\n| Elastic\n~90 | Depth jump from 12–16 in | 5 × 3 | 15 | 2 min\n| | Single-leg lateral hop, continuous | 4 × 5 / side | 40 | 90 s\n| | Continuous broad jump, 3 reps | 4 × 3 jumps | 24 | 90 s\n| | Bound for height | 3 × 4 | 12 | 90 s\n\n- Watch distance, not set count. If any jump drops more than ~5% off your first broad jump, the session is over.\n- Every rep maximal or near it. If you’re breathing hard, you’re doing conditioning.\n- Cut, don’t skip. After a hard lifting week, halve the contacts.\n- Counting contacts: one landing = one contact, even on two feet. The volumes are calibrated that way.\n\nProgressing between blocks. Add contacts before you add intensity. Raise intensity by shortening ground contact time, not by jumping higher or adding box height. If your broad jump hasn’t moved after two full blocks, the limiter is usually recovery or strength, not jump volume.\n\n## Plyo exercise library\nSetup, execution, cues and the errors that matter. Tap an exercise to open it. The same entries open from Thursday’s card.\n\nWarm-up drills\n\n### Pogo hop\n\nSetup. Stand tall, feet hip width, arms at your sides or lightly bent. Weight on the balls of your feet.\nExecution. Hop straight up repeatedly using only the ankles. Knees stay nearly straight and barely bend. Height is small, two to four inches. The goal is the shortest possible time on the ground, not air time.\nCues. Think “stiff spring.” Toes pulled up toward your shin before you land so the forefoot contacts first. The rhythm should sound fast and even.\nCommon errors. Bending the knees to create height, which turns it into a squat jump and removes the ankle stimulus. Landing flat-footed or heel-first. Letting the rhythm slow as the set goes on.\nThe lowest-stress drill in the program, and the one that most directly builds the Achilles and calf stiffness everything else relies on.\n\n### Ankle skip\n\nSetup. Standing, ready to travel forward over about 20 meters.\nExecution. Skip forward driving off the ankle with a minimal knee bend, opposite arm swinging. Cover ground with quick, light contacts rather than big bounds.\nCues. Light and quick. Contacts should be barely audible.\nCommon errors. Turning it into a powerful A-skip with high knee drive. That is a different drill and adds fatigue you do not want before the main sets.\n\nJump tests\n\n### Broad jump (single effort)\n\nSetup. Feet hip to shoulder width behind a line. Clear landing space of at least 3 meters. A flat, non-slip surface: grass or rubber gym flooring is ideal, concrete is the worst option.\nExecution. Hinge and load by dipping to roughly a quarter squat, swinging the arms back. Jump forward and slightly up, driving the arms forward and fully extending hips, knees and ankles. Land on both feet with hips back and knees tracking over toes, then absorb and stick: no extra hop, no stagger.\nCues. Reach, don’t fall. Drive the arms hard; they contribute more distance than most people expect. Land like you are catching yourself in a quarter squat, not a deep one.\nCommon errors. Landing stiff-legged with straight knees (jarring and risky). Knees collapsing inward on landing. Piking at the waist in the air. Taking a step after landing. If you cannot stick it, you jumped past your current ability to absorb it.\nYour measured lift for the block. Mark the takeoff line and measure to your rearmost heel. Log the best jump of each session.\n\n### Vertical jump\n\nSetup. Stand side on to a wall. Reach up with the near arm, feet flat, and mark the highest point your fingers reach: that is your standing reach. Chalk, tape or a coach’s eye all work; a Vertec or a wall you can mark is easiest.\nExecution. From a standstill, dip to about a quarter squat, swing both arms down and back, then jump as high as you can and touch the wall at the top with the near hand. No run-up and no step into it. Three attempts, resting fully between them, and keep the best.\nCues. Down fast, up faster: the dip should be quick and shallow. Throw the arms up as you extend. Reach at the top of the jump, not on the way up.\nCommon errors. Dipping too deep or too slowly, which loses the stretch reflex. Taking a step in. Measuring the jump touch but forgetting the standing reach, which is the number you subtract.\nJump height is the touch height minus your standing reach. Record the same way every time — same wall, same shoes, same arm — or the comparison is meaningless.\n\n### Standing triple jump\n\nSetup. A line to start behind and about 10 meters of flat, forgiving space. Grass or rubber flooring; do not test this on concrete. Warm up fully first: it is the highest-force test in the set.\nExecution. From a two-foot standstill, jump forward and land on one foot (the hop), immediately bound forward onto the opposite foot (the step), then jump off that foot and land on both feet (the jump). Three linked efforts, no pause between them. Measure from the line to your rearmost heel on the final landing.\nCues. Even, rhythmic, flowing. Aim for three jumps of similar length rather than an enormous first one that leaves you nothing to land on. Arms drive on every contact.\nCommon errors. Attacking the hop so hard that the step and jump collapse. Pausing between contacts, which turns it into three standing jumps. Reaching for distance on the last landing and falling backwards — stick it or the attempt does not count.\nThe most demanding jump test here, and the one that exposes single-leg elasticity and left-to-right differences the broad jump hides. Skip it if an ankle or Achilles is complaining.\n\nExtensive\n\n### Broad jump (single effort)\n\nSetup. Feet hip to shoulder width behind a line. Clear landing space of at least 3 meters. A flat, non-slip surface: grass or rubber gym flooring is ideal, concrete is the worst option.\nExecution. Hinge and load by dipping to roughly a quarter squat, swinging the arms back. Jump forward and slightly up, driving the arms forward and fully extending hips, knees and ankles. Land on both feet with hips back and knees tracking over toes, then absorb and stick: no extra hop, no stagger.\nCues. Reach, don’t fall. Drive the arms hard; they contribute more distance than most people expect. Land like you are catching yourself in a quarter squat, not a deep one.\nCommon errors. Landing stiff-legged with straight knees (jarring and risky). Knees collapsing inward on landing. Piking at the waist in the air. Taking a step after landing. If you cannot stick it, you jumped past your current ability to absorb it.\nYour measured lift for the block. Mark the takeoff line and measure to your rearmost heel. Log the best jump of each session.\n\n### Box jump, step down\n\nSetup. A stable box or plyo platform at a height you can land on in a quarter squat, not a deep one: typically 18 to 24 inches. Stand about a foot back.\nExecution. Dip, swing the arms, and jump onto the box, landing softly on both feet with hips slightly back. Stand fully upright on top, then step down one foot at a time.\nCues. Soft, quiet landing. If you hear a thud, you are landing too heavily or the box is too high.\nCommon errors. Choosing a box so tall you have to tuck your knees to your chest to clear it. That measures hip flexibility, not power. Jumping down off the box, which adds eccentric load with none of the benefit.\nAlways step down. Repeated jump-downs are where box jump injuries come from, and they add contacts you did not program.\n\n### Lateral bound (stick 2 seconds)\n\nSetup. Stand on one leg. Clear space to your side. Non-slip footing matters more here than anywhere else.\nExecution. Push laterally off the standing leg and land on the opposite leg. Absorb on that single leg with a soft knee and the hip loaded, and hold the landing completely still for a two-count before bounding back the other way.\nCues. Land with the knee tracking over the middle of the foot and the hip taking the load. The two-second hold is the exercise: it trains deceleration and frontal-plane control.\nCommon errors. Rushing the hold. Letting the knee cave inward. Landing on a stiff, straight leg. Bounding so far sideways you cannot control the landing. Distance is capped by your ability to stick it.\n\nUnilateral + reactive\n\n### Continuous broad jump\n\nSetup. Same as the broad jump, with space for two to three consecutive jumps.\nExecution. Perform a broad jump, and the instant you land, immediately jump again with minimal time on the ground. The landing of jump one is the loading for jump two.\nCues. Rebound, don’t reset. The whole point is that ground contact is short. Slightly shorter individual jumps with fast contacts beat long jumps with a pause.\nCommon errors. Pausing to re-set between reps, which makes it separate single jumps. Sinking into a deep squat on each landing, which kills the elastic stimulus.\n\n### Single-leg hop (straight line)\n\nSetup. Balance on one leg, hands free. Flat surface with several meters ahead.\nExecution. Hop forward on the same leg repeatedly, aiming for consistent distance and short contacts. Complete all reps on one leg, then switch.\nCues. Stay tall through the torso. Hips level: do not let the opposite hip drop on each landing.\nCommon errors. Letting distance shrink through the set. Excessive side-to-side drift. Hunching forward at the waist.\nCompare left to right. A noticeable asymmetry in distance or landing control is worth addressing with unilateral strength work before adding jump volume.\n\n### Bound for distance\n\nSetup. Space of 20 to 30 meters. Start with a few walking steps into the first bound rather than from a dead stop.\nExecution. Alternate legs in exaggerated running strides, driving off one leg and landing on the other, covering as much ground per contact as possible. Opposite arm drives forward with each bound.\nCues. Push the ground behind you and reach with the landing leg. Long, powerful, rhythmic.\nCommon errors. Turning it into ordinary running. Landing heel-first. Leaning back to try to hang in the air.\n\n### Hurdle hop (low, continuous)\n\nSetup. Three to five low hurdles, cones, or 6 to 12 inch objects in a line, roughly a meter apart. Use objects that fall over harmlessly if clipped.\nExecution. Hop over each with both feet, landing and immediately rebounding into the next with minimal ground contact. Complete the line, walk back, repeat.\nCues. Minimal time on the ground. Land on the balls of the feet, ankles stiff.\nCommon errors. Hurdles too high, which forces a knee tuck and a long pause on each landing. Pausing between hurdles to re-set.\nIf you only have one or two objects, use fewer and reset between passes rather than raising the height.\n\nElastic\n\n### Depth jump\n\nSetup. Box 12 to 16 inches high. Stand at the edge. Landing area must be flat and non-slip with room to jump forward or up.\nExecution. Step off the box. Do not jump off or down. Land on both feet on the balls of the feet and, as fast as humanly possible, rebound into a maximal vertical jump. The time between landing and takeoff is the entire exercise.\nCues. Land and leave. Imagine the floor is hot. Target roughly a quarter-second on the ground; if you sink into a deep squat before jumping, the box is too high.\nCommon errors. Jumping off the box instead of stepping, which changes the landing force unpredictably. Using too high a box: the single most common error, and it converts an elastic drill into a heavy eccentric one. Pausing on landing.\nHighest-stress movement in the program. If your vertical rebound off the box is lower than a normal standing vertical jump, the box is too high. Lower it. Start at 12 inches even if that feels trivially easy.\n\n### Single-leg lateral hop (continuous)\n\nSetup. Balance on one leg. Optionally use a line or low object to hop over.\nExecution. Hop side to side on the same leg, continuously, with short ground contacts. No pause between reps.\nCues. Small and fast beats big and slow. Knee tracks over the foot on every contact.\nCommon errors. Knee caving inward as fatigue sets in: stop the set when you see it. Landing flat-footed.\nThe highest frontal-plane demand in the program, and the most protective for ankles and knees.\n\n### Bound for height\n\nSetup. Same as bounding for distance, with the same run-in.\nExecution. Alternate-leg bounds emphasizing vertical displacement rather than horizontal: drive the knee up and get as high as you can per contact, covering less ground.\nCues. Punch the knee up and the arms up. Think “up” on every contact.\nCommon errors. Drifting back into distance bounding once you fatigue. Collapsing into a deep knee bend on landing.\n\nUpper body (optional)\n\n### Overhead med ball slam\n\nSetup. Feet hip to shoulder width, a non-bouncy slam ball of 8–15 lb. Floor you do not mind hitting.\nExecution. Reach the ball overhead with the whole body long, then throw it into the floor in front of your feet as hard as you can, folding at the hips and ribs. Pick it up, reset, repeat.\nCues. Throw the ball through the floor, not at it. The power comes from the hips and trunk, not the arms.\nCommon errors. Bending only at the arms. Rushing the reset so the set turns into conditioning. Using a bouncy ball, which takes the finish away from you.\nIntent is the whole exercise. If the ball is heavy enough to slow you down, it is too heavy.\n\n### Backward scoop toss\n\nSetup. Feet slightly wider than the hips, ball held low between the legs, clear space behind you.\nExecution. Dip into a quarter squat and throw the ball up and behind you, extending hips, knees and ankles fully.\nCues. One long push from the floor to the fingertips. Let it pull you onto your toes.\nCommon errors. Squatting deep and turning it into a lift. Throwing with the back rather than the legs.\n\n### Single-arm dumbbell snatch\n\nSetup. One moderate dumbbell or kettlebell between the feet, feet a little wider than the hips, chest up.\nExecution. Hinge, then drive the floor away and pull the weight straight up past the ribs, punching the hand through overhead as the elbow turns over. Lock the arm out with the weight over the shoulder. Lower it down the same path and reset on the floor between reps.\nCues. Legs and hips throw it, the arm only guides it. One clean line close to the body.\nCommon errors. Swinging it out in an arc and catching it on a bent, soft arm. Chasing reps for breath: this is a power set, not a circuit.\nThe most total-body option here, and the closest thing in the block to an Olympic lift without needing the technique.\n\n### Explosive high pull\n\nSetup. A barbell at about 40–50% of your deadlift, or a pair of dumbbells. Start with the bar just above the knee rather than the floor.\nExecution. Push the floor away, extend the hips hard, and let that speed carry the bar up the body to about sternum height with the elbows high and outside. Lower it back to the start under control.\nCues. The hips throw it; the arms only finish the ride. Bar stays close, elbows finish above the hands.\nCommon errors. Turning it into an upright row by pulling early with the arms. Going heavy enough that the bar never gets above the navel. Leaning back at the top.\n\n### Med ball chest pass\n\nSetup. A meter or two from a solid wall, ball at the sternum, athletic stance.\nExecution. Push the ball into the wall as fast as you can, catch the rebound and reset. Feet stay planted.\nCues. Fast hands. Think of pushing the wall away rather than throwing at it.\nCommon errors. Standing so close that the rebound arrives before you are set. Turning it into a rep count instead of a speed drill.\n\n### Plyometric push-up\n\nSetup. Push-up position on a forgiving surface. Hands under the shoulders.\nExecution. Lower under control, then drive up hard enough that the hands leave the floor. Land with soft elbows and go straight into the next rep only if you can keep the height.\nCues. Short time on the floor, ribs down, body in one line. Height over reps.\nCommon errors. Letting the hips sag. Grinding out reps once the push-off slows, which is where shoulders get sore.\nThe highest-stress movement in this block. Leave it out on weeks where the bench already feels heavy.\n\n### Push-up onto plates or low boxes\n\nSetup. Two 25 lb plates, low boxes or steps, a little wider than shoulder width, with the floor between them.\nExecution. From the floor, push up hard enough to land both hands on top of the plates, then lower back down between them and repeat. Or start on top and drop into the gap.\nCues. Land with the elbows soft and the body still in one line. Height and control over speed.\nCommon errors. Plates too far apart, which throws the shoulders into a wide catch. Piking the hips to get airborne.\nEasier on the wrists and shoulders than clapping push-ups, and it gives the drop a defined height.\n\n### Speed bench or floor press\n\nSetup. Bar at about 50–60% of your bench max, or a pair of dumbbells you could press 15 times. A spotter or safeties if you are on a bench.\nExecution. Lower under control to the chest, pause for nothing, and drive the bar up as fast as you possibly can. Three reps, then rack it. Speed decides the set, never the weight.\nCues. Accelerate all the way through: the bar should feel like it wants to leave your hands at the top. Reset your brace before every rep.\nCommon errors. Creeping the weight up until it stops moving fast, which turns this back into ordinary benching. Bouncing the bar off the chest. Running the reps together.\nThe option that needs no new equipment. Because it is the same pattern as your work sets, it is also the one most likely to cost you on Friday: keep it light.\n\n### Explosive pull-up\n\nSetup. A bar you can do at least five clean pull-ups on. Full hang, no kipping.\nExecution. From a dead hang, pull as violently as you can and aim to bring the chest to the bar, or to release the hands at the top if the bar and your grip allow it. Lower under control, re-hang, and reset for the next rep.\nCues. Every rep starts from a still hang. The aim is speed off the bottom, not getting the chin over by any means.\nCommon errors. Swinging the legs to generate the pull. Grinding out reps once the speed goes: the set ends when the bar stops coming fast.\nThe only explosive pulling in the program, and it pairs well with the pull-up progression you are already running.\n\n### Explosive band row\n\nSetup. A band anchored at chest height, standing far enough back that it is under tension with your arms straight.\nExecution. Snap both elbows back to the ribs as fast as you can, then let the band pull the arms straight again under control. Reset your stance between reps if it drags you forward.\nCues. Fast in, slow out. Shoulder blades move, the torso does not.\nCommon errors. Leaning back to help. Band so heavy that the pull turns slow, which makes it strength work instead.\n\n### Rotational med ball throw\n\nSetup. Side on to a wall, feet shoulder width, ball at the hip furthest from it.\nExecution. Turn the back hip through and release the ball into the wall across your body. Complete all reps on one side, then switch.\nCues. Hips lead, arms follow. The back heel should come off the floor as you turn.\nCommon errors. Throwing with the arms while the hips stay square. Taking the ball too far behind the body and losing the whip.\nThe only rotation in a week of squats, presses and riding. Worth keeping even when you cut everything else.\n\n### Band rotational punch\n\nSetup. A band anchored at chest height behind you, side on, both hands on the handle at the ribs.\nExecution. Turn the back hip through and punch the hands across the body as fast as you can, then return under control. All reps on one side, then switch.\nCues. Hips lead, arms follow, back heel turns. Same pattern as the med ball throw with no ball and no wall.\nCommon errors. Rotating with the arms while the feet stay stuck. Letting the band snap you back around rather than controlling the return.\nThe travel-friendly rotation option: one band and a door anchor.\n\n## Programs\nYour strength template and your conditioning protocol are separate choices, set in Setup → Program, and the week is built from the two. That is how the books present it: Tactical Barbell II's sample weeks are headed “Black + Zulu”, “Black + Operator”, “Black + Fighter”.\n\n| Strength | Days | What it is\n| Operator | 3 | Three full-body days a week, the same cluster every session, never near failure. The default, and what Black is built around.\n| Zulu | 4 | Four shorter days a week. The cluster is split in two and each half runs twice, which leaves far more room for assistance work than Operator does.\n| Gladiator | 4 | A specialist, not a staple — the book says use it sparingly. Four sessions a week over two pairs of lifts, high frequency and high volume, finishing on a peak set of each.\n| StrongLifts 5×5 | 3 | For a true beginner, and the only program here with no percentages in it. Three days a week, two alternating sessions, five sets of five. The weight goes up every session you finish and comes down 10% when you miss three in a row. It runs until it stops working rather than in cycles — which, for a beginner, is usually a few months.\n| Mass | 4 | Tactical Barbell III’s headline template, and Operator’s forefather. Bench, squat and the weighted pull-up three times a week on alternate days; the deadlift gets a day of its own at the end. Hypertrophy first, strength alongside it.\n| Specificity Alpha | 4 | A three-week hypertrophy block that keeps heavy work in the mix. Two maximal-strength days on bench, squat and deadlift, and two days of your own cluster at higher reps. The book wants it after a stretch of General Mass.\n| Specificity Bravo | 4 | Three weeks of nothing but hypertrophy. Eight to sixteen exercises split in two, each half trained twice a week at 50 to 75%. Dropping maximal strength for a block is the point of it, not an oversight.\n| Old Warhorse | 3 | Four blocks that start deliberately light and force the numbers up rather than testing for them. Operator’s three days a week, but the volume falls and the sets tighten block by block: high and flexible at the start, low and fixed by the end. For experienced lifters who find heavy singles expensive to recover from.\n| Back Break · squat only | 3 | A block that gives your back a rest without giving up lower-body work. Bench and the pull-up run as usual; you squat once a week, on the last day, working up to your best set for that day. Deadlift-style accessories carry the posterior chain on the other two days.\n| Back Break · deadlift only | 3 | The same idea for the pull. Bench and the pull-up as usual, one deadlift day a week worked up to your best set, and goblet squats, double-kettlebell front squats or pistols on the other two days to keep the legs honest.\n| Grey Man | 3 | Two main lifts a day, then supplementary work of your own choosing. Alternates A-B-A one week and B-A-B the next, which leaves four days clear for conditioning and recovery.\n| Fighter HT | 3 | Mass building for the minimalist. Squat and bench twice a week, deadlift on its own day, five sets of everything. Three-week blocks, and week three finishes with an all-out set.\n| Fighter | 2 | The two-day template. Same idea as Operator on half the lifting days, for when something else — skills, running, shift work — is taking the time.\n\n| Conditioning | Sessions | What it is\n| Black | 3 | The standard continuation protocol. Strength and high-intensity conditioning weighted equally, with endurance kept ticking over.\n| Green | 4 | Endurance first. Easy aerobic work most days, a strength-endurance circuit each week, and one hard session every other week. Built for rucking, selection prep and long events. The book runs it alongside two lifting days, so it sits best with Fighter.\n| Green · TB III | 3 | Three easy sessions a week while you build mass. Long, low intensity, and no intervals — the book picks these specifically because they do not work against hypertrophy. Pairs with the General Mass templates: Mass, Gladiator and Fighter HT.\n| Black · TB III | 2 | Two short, maximal sessions a week during a Specificity block. Sprints rather than intervals: the book picks them because brief all-out efforts drive an anabolic response instead of working against one. The book says to keep them off lifting days.\n| Green · Progressive SE | 6 | The no-barbell variation. Three strength-endurance circuits and two easy aerobic sessions a week, climbing over a three-week block. For when you have no equipment — the book says not to run it past nine weeks without going back to maximal strength.\n| None | 0 | Lifting only. No conditioning is scheduled, and deload and test weeks leave out their easy cardio too. You can still add a session of your own on any day.\n\nOne of these is not from the books. StrongLifts 5×5 is there because the four books have no answer for somebody who has never squatted: Tactical Barbell assumes you arrive with maxes to take percentages of, and Base Building is conditioning with maintenance lifting under it, not a strength program. Where every other template here can be checked against a page, that one is labeled ours and quoted nowhere.\n\n- The lifting days go where the book's table puts them. Operator on days 1, 3 and 5, Fighter on 1 and 4, Zulu on 1, 2, 4 and 5. Zulu's pairs sit together on purpose — A and B work different lifts.\n\n- Conditioning fills what is left, spread out where the week allows. That is a preference, not a rule: TB II says plainly that “your HIC and E can be done on any day … you can do two days in a row, or whatever. As long as you’re hitting your weekly minimums.” So the app arranges them apart and lets you put them together, with a note rather than a refusal.\n\n- The default layout follows the book’s own principle for arranging a week: “It’s not set in stone. As long as you adhere to the principles — one rest day. No back to back same sessions. Completed within 7 days.” So a protocol lays its week out with the strength days apart. Zulu’s A and B are different sessions, so they are allowed to touch, and are meant to.\n\n- Moving a session is one thing and it is never refused. Pick the day, and that session happens on that day. Nothing else moves — not the day you took it from, not the day it lands on, not the rest of the week. A day can hold two sessions or none, and a day with none is a rest day. Move it into next week if that is when you will do it.\n\n- You get told, not stopped. Two lifting sessions on one day, or on consecutive days, is the one thing the book asks you not to do, so the app says so — do the heavier one first and cut the accessories on the second. It is a remark, not a veto: the week you can actually train beats the week that reads correctly.\n\n- If it will not fit, a session rides along after the barbell work rather than taking your rest day. Four lifting days and three conditioning sessions is seven, so Zulu + Black puts a HIC on day 1 — exactly what the book's own Black + Zulu week does.\n\n- Specificity is a block, not a program. Alpha and Bravo come from Tactical Barbell III and run three weeks at a time, four days a week on days 1, 2, 4 and 5. The book wants them after a stretch of General Mass, and says to bridge between blocks. Alpha keeps two maximal-strength days (bench, squat and deadlift — the deadlift at one work set per session) against two hypertrophy days; Bravo drops the barbell work entirely and trains nothing but the cluster, which is the point of it rather than an oversight. Bravo also runs the back half of its week five percent heavier than the front.\n\n- A hypertrophy day has no barbell prescription. The whole session is your H cluster, so it shows up under Supplementary rather than as a list of lifts: six to twelve exercises for Alpha, eight to sixteen for Bravo, split in half with each half on its own day. What goes in them is yours — the book gives sample clusters and leaves the choice open. The percentage is of your own best for that movement.\n\n- Tactical Barbell III brings its own conditioning. The book is blunt about it — use these sessions, not TB II's Green and Black — because while mass is the objective, conditioning is there to keep you operational rather than to build an elite base. Green · TB III is three easy sessions a week (a ruck, a recovery run, and Endurance Predator) and goes with the General Mass templates. Black · TB III is two short maximal ones (Anabolic Sprints, then RESET-20 and Hill Sprints alternating) and goes with Specificity. Both numbers are the book's caps, and the app schedules the cap.\n\n- Black sessions stay off lifting days, which the book asks for outright. Green ones may go on either.\n\n- Ageless Athlete does not add a different program — the book asks outright whether an older athlete needs one and answers no. What changes is the dosing: train off a training max, take a recovery week every fourth week, and test only when you expect to learn something. Setup has those as a preset. It does add two templates of its own, below.\n\n- Old Warhorse is four blocks that start deliberately light and force the numbers up rather than testing for them. Operator's three days and percentages throughout; what moves is the volume — 5 to 10 sets in block 1, then 4 to 8, then 4 to 5, then 3 to 4 — and whether the sets are flexible (I/A) or fixed. Between blocks you add 2 to 5% to the training max, which the book leaves to your judgment, so the app tells you rather than deciding. Block 4 finishes on a 3-rep max at 100 to 107%.\n\n- Back “Break” is for a block when your spine wants a rest. One lower-body lift, once a week, on the last day: squat only or deadlift only. You do not work off a percentage — you climb to the best set you have that day, then take optional down sets at 80, 85 or 90% of it depending on the week. Upper body runs Operator as usual, and the other two days carry accessories from the opposite pattern. The book is clear that it is not the best way to progress a squat or deadlift, but it might be the breather that keeps you training.\n\n- The recovery week that 3-1-3-1 inserts is not an off week. No barbells and no HIC — that is what recharges the nervous system, joints and hormones — but two easy sessions, kept easy, plus anything you already do and as much mobility as you like. On the last lifting day before one, the app offers the in-block check: one heavy single above 95%, or a rep max, instead of a whole test week.\n\n- A travel week is a week-long pause you add from the Plan tab, like Base Building but for a hotel: three sessions of dumbbell and bodyweight work with rep targets instead of percentages, conditioning and plyos unchanged. It touches none of your maxes, and the end-of-cycle review ignores it because it is not a cycle week. The movements are yours, in Setup.\n\n- Starting over. Archiving the current program draws a line under it and begins another — carrying your maxes across, or going back through the bridge week to retest them. Anything archived stays readable from History, Status and the Plan tab, and personal records and conditioning bests still count across every program you have run.\n\n- Switching re-draws this week and the ones ahead. Weeks you have already trained keep the program they were done under. Each strength template keeps its own wave, so you can switch across and back without losing tuned numbers.\n\n## Base Building\nTactical Barbell II's Block I: eight weeks of aerobic base and strength-endurance before you go back to heavy barbell work. Add it from the Plan tab and it runs in place of your next cycle; Operator picks up where it left off afterwards, at the same maxes.\n\n| Day | W1 | W2 | W3 | W4 | W5 | W6 | W7 | W8\n| 1 | SE 3×20 | SE 3×30 | SE 3×40 | SE 1×50 | SE 3×50 | Strength | Strength | Strength\n| 2 | E 30M | E 40M | E 50M | E 60M | E 45–60M | HIC | HIC | HIC\n| 3 | E 30M | E 40M | E 50M | E 60M | E 45–60M | Recovery | Recovery | Recovery\n| 4 | SE 2×20 | SE 2×30 | SE 2×40 | SE 1×50 | SE 2×50 | Strength | Strength | Strength\n| 5 | Recovery | Recovery | Recovery | Recovery | Recovery | HIC | HIC | HIC\n| 6 | E 35–120M | E 45–120M | E 55–120M | E 60–120M | E 45–120M | E 30–60M | E 30–60M | E 30–60M\n| 7 | Rest | Rest | Rest | Rest | Rest | Rest | Rest | Rest\n\n- Weeks 1–5 are endurance and strength-endurance dense, with no barbell work at all. That is deliberate: it frees the energy for the long sessions and gives tendons and joints a break before heavy lifting returns.\n- Weeks 6–8 taper the long work and bring back two strength days and the aerobic-leaning HIC sessions.\n- Every E session is a minimum of 30 minutes. Day 6 is the long one, with a recovery day before it and a rest day after, so push the duration there.\n- Recovery days are movement drills, mobility, an easy swim or bike, a walk, yoga, or nothing at all.\n- At least one completely free day a week. Walking and pick-up sport are fine.\n- Eat for it. The book asks for 1–2 g of protein per pound of bodyweight through the block and is firmly against going low-carb while the volume is up.\n- Strength-first reverses it: lift for the first five weeks, strength-endurance for the last three. The book doesn't give rep numbers for those late SE weeks, so the app ramps 3×30, 3×40, 3×50.\n\nTactical Barbell III runs its own version, and it is a different block. Six weeks instead of eight, and only two domains: strength-endurance three times a week on alternate days, endurance on the days between. No barbell strength week, no HIC, no recovery day. The book is explicit that this is not the TB II model — mass is the objective and conditioning is there to keep you operational, not to build an elite base.\n\n| Day | W1 | W2 | W3 | W4 | W5 | W6\n| 1 | SE 3×10 @ 15% | SE 3×10 @ 15% | SE 3×10 @ 15% | SE 3×10 @ 20% | SE 3×10 @ 20% | SE 3×10 @ 20%\n| 2 | E 30M | E 30M | E 30M | E 30M | E 30M | E 30M\n| 3 | SE 3×20 @ 20% | SE 3×20 @ 20% | SE 3×20 @ 20% | SE 3×20 @ 25% | SE 3×20 @ 25% | SE 3×20 @ 25%\n| 4 | E 30M | E 30M | E 30M | E 30M | E 30M | E 30M\n| 5 | SE 3×30 @ 25% | SE 3×30 @ 25% | SE 3×30 @ 25% | SE 3×30 @ 30% | SE 3×30 @ 30% | SE 3×30 @ 30%\n| 6 | E 30M | E 30M | E 30M | E 30M | E 30M | E 30M\n| 7 | Rest | Rest | Rest | Rest | Rest | Rest\n\n- The reps climb inside the week, not across it. Ten, twenty, thirty on the three SE days, and weeks 4 to 6 add five percent to each load. Every week has the same shape.\n\n- E is capped at thirty minutes. Not a minimum — a cap. The book says “no more, no less”, and answers the obvious question about longer sessions with “the short answer is NO”: more endurance than this works against the thing you came for.\n\n- Low intensity throughout. Talk test, or 120 to 150 bpm, or nose breathing. No intervals and no sprints for the full six weeks. Walking or riding counts if thirty minutes of running is a lot.\n\n- The cluster is the General Mass lifts — bench, squat, Romanian deadlift and abs — so the base you build is for the block you are about to run. Pick TB III barbell on the day. The book says to keep the conventional deadlift out of it: light and high-rep, it is an awkward lift.\n\n- Add it from the Plan tab, the same way as the other two.\n\n## Strength-endurance circuits\nFive to eight exercises covering the whole body, done one after another. Do your reps, take a short rest, move to the next. After a full circuit, rest two minutes and go again. The shorter you can make the rests, the better — down to none if you can hold it together.\n\n- Reps run 20 to 50. If you can't get them all in one go, rest-pause until they're done, then move on. Failing at 25 now and hitting 40 later is the whole point.\n- Barbells and dumbbells: roughly 15–30% of your one-rep max. Don't test for it. Too heavy, take weight off.\n- Pick movements you can reach without queueing. A busy bench breaks the rests that make this work.\n- Avoid anything you can't do for high reps. A rule of thumb: be good for 15–20 reps before putting a movement in. Pull-ups, pistols and one-arm push-ups sit in that gray area.\n- One arm or one leg at a time splits the reps: 30 means 15 a side.\n\nTB III barbell\n\n- Bench press\n- Squat\n- Romanian deadlift\n- Abs\nThe book’s cluster for TB III Base Building: the same lifts General Mass runs, so the base you build is the one you are about to need. It says not to use the conventional deadlift here — it is an awkward movement light and for high reps.\n\nBodyweight\n\n- Push-ups\n- Squats\n- Kipping pull-ups or inverted rows\n- Bicycle crunches\n- Dips\n- Back extensions\nAdd a weight vest or a pack with 5–10 lb to make it harder.\n\nBarbell\n\n- Push press\n- Front squat\n- Row\n- Bench or floor press\n- Shrugs\n- Romanian deadlift\nOne bar, one light weight, the whole circuit. An Olympic bar with a pair of 10s is the right idea.\n\nKettlebell\n\n- Swings\n- Goblet squat\n- Renegade rows\n- Single-arm floor press\n- Kettlebell deadlift\nAnything one arm or one leg at a time splits the reps: 30 means 15 a side.\n\nDumbbell\n\n- Bench or floor press\n- Lunges\n- Rows\n- Dumbbell push press\n- Squats\n- Lying leg raises\nA pair of light dumbbells for everything except the leg raises.\n\nSet your own in Setup → Strength-endurance, or switch cluster on any single SE day from the session card.\n\n## Accessories\n\nJobs, not a list. Each lifting day covers a few jobs — a horizontal pull, some core, a carry, arms — and you choose what does each one. Setup → Accessories sets which jobs a day covers and what fills them. The choice is per cycle, like your cluster lifts, because that is what it is: a program decision, not something to re-make every session. It is also per day, so a job that comes round three times a week can be a different movement each time.\n\nSwapping on the day. If your biceps slot is barbell curls and the bar is taken, change it on the day’s card. That session uses what you picked and is logged as it, the block’s choice is untouched, and the card marks it today only so you can see at a glance that it was a substitution.\n\nIn session mode. Each job is its own step, after the lifts: it names what is doing it, lets you swap for that session, and takes the sets.\n\nLogging. Tick a set off and three things happen: the set is logged, the rest starts, and the next set is filled in with the weight and reps you just did — which is what the next set usually is. Both numbers stay editable until you tick that one too, anything you have already typed is left alone, and adding a set copies the one before it the same way. Or just Mark done if you would rather not count at all. Everything is recorded against the job and against the exercise, so a year later the log still says it was hammer curls and not what happens to be in that slot now.\n\nThe rest between sets is yours to set, and it is per job rather than one figure for all of them — a loaded carry and a set of curls do not want the same clock. It sits with the sets, in the day’s card and in session mode, and starts at 90 seconds, where the main lifts use your per-lift two to five minutes. A superset gets two clocks: a short one between its halves — 20 seconds by default, enough to walk to the other bar — and then its own rest once both halves are ticked. Set Between the pair to Off if you would rather go straight into the second movement.\n\nMissing a movement? The list is shared and curated, so ask and it can be added for everyone. A movement that is retired stops being offered but stays with anyone who had already chosen it — nobody has their program rewritten mid-cycle.\n\nThe order is not the order you listed them in. Core runs last and carries run last of all, whatever position they hold in Setup, and a superset containing either moves as a pair rather than being split. A fried grip makes everything after it worse, and bracing is the thing you want intact while you are still loading a bar.\n\nThe ab triad is a core option that is one prescription rather than three exercises: it is taken exactly as written, and nothing is added to it.\n\n- After the main lifts, never before. Two or three sets of each job, a couple of reps short of failure.\n- Skip entirely on heavy weeks and deloads.\n- Legs need almost nothing. Keep the pull-up progression in.\n\n## Starting from nothing · StrongLifts 5×5\n\nWho it is for. Anybody who does not yet have a one-rep max worth taking a percentage of. Every other program here needs one; this one needs the opposite — a bar light enough that the first few weeks are easy.\n\nWhat it is. Three days a week, two sessions alternating. A is squat, bench and row; B is squat, press and deadlift. Five sets of five on everything but the deadlift, which is one set of five — five sets of deadlift three times a week is the one part of the published program nobody actually runs. A-B-A one week, B-A-B the next, so neither session always lands on a Monday.\n\nHow the weight moves. Finish every set and it goes up next session: 5 lb on the lifts, 10 on the deadlift. Miss any set and it stays where it is. Miss 3 sessions in a row on the same lift and it comes down 10%, rounded to the nearest plate, and you climb back up — the second run at a weight usually goes through. All of that is read back out of what you logged, so a week you missed entirely costs you nothing: the program waits rather than moving on without you.\n\nNo cycles, no deloads, no retest. There is nothing to test — the weight on the bar already is the test, every session. There is nothing to deload from either: the reset is the deload, and it arrives when you have earned it rather than on week six. So the program runs open-ended, and the weeks are numbered straight through.\n\nWhen to stop. When a lift has reset twice. That is normally the end of the novice phase: adding weight every session has stopped working, and the answer is a program that does not try to. The app says so on the card when it happens and points at Operator. Ours — the published program keeps going and changes the set scheme instead; we would rather hand you to a program built for where you now are.\n\nAssistance work. None, on purpose, and the program says to add none until the main lifts stall. Both days start with no accessory jobs and the picker is right there if you disagree; chin-ups and dips are the program's own optional additions.\n\nConditioning still composes. Pick any protocol alongside it, the same as any other template. That is this app's arrangement rather than the program's — StrongLifts says nothing either way, and the Tactical Barbell idea that strength and conditioning run together is the reason the app exists.\n\n## After the session\n\nThree questions at three different grains, and each one feeds something.\n\nEvery heavy set: how did it move? Fast, Solid, Ground — the books' own words for it. Tap one on the last set you ticked, or rate it in session mode. This is the best evidence the app has about whether a max has room in it: with four or more rated heavy sets the end-of-cycle review decides on those rather than on your session RPE, and three in four moving fast is a heavy week that did not look heavy.\n\nEvery session: an RPE, and a grinder flag on any set that stalled. A grinder at 70 to 80% means the max is too high — the book's own rule, not a judgement call.\n\nEvery lifting session: how it felt, and whether anything hurt. Easy, about right or hard; and nothing, a niggle or sharp pain, with where. A niggle is only ever reported back to you. Sharp pain is the one answer that outranks every other rule. Once on a lift and the review holds that max instead of adding to it; twice and it comes down, with the joint named. It beats the grinder count, the RPE and the fast-set count, and it applies even when there are too few sessions to judge on otherwise. An averaged score can be argued with; a joint cannot.\n\nThe other half of this is asked before you start rather than after — see What matters today, above.\n\n## What the app works out for you\n\nThe end-of-cycle review. At the end of every block each lift gets a suggested next max — lower 5%, hold, the standard increase, or a bigger jump — from your grinders, missed sets, per-set effort on the heavy weeks, average readiness and any pain logged. It shows the arithmetic it used, and nothing is applied until you approve it, lift by lift.\n\nThe weekly debrief arrives on a Monday and reads the week you have just finished: what you did, what you missed, and what is worth noticing before you repeat it. The end-of-cycle report takes the whole block, and the six-month report puts where you are now against where you were half a year ago.\n\nThe coach answers questions about your own log and can quote the books at you. It only ever suggests: nothing it says changes your program until you do it yourself.\n\nStatus is the same thing without the words — position in the cycle, adherence, strength and conditioning trends, bodyweight and the burn estimate, personal records, the climbing total, your pull-up rung and your benchmark.\n\n## Supplements\n\nWhat the books actually recommend, and nothing invented. Level 1 is what they call worth taking; level 2 is optional on top. Setup → Supplements keeps the list and what you are taking.\n\nLevel 1\n\n| Supplement | Dose | Source\n| Creatine monohydrate\nhighly recommended | 5 g a day in TB1, 5 to 10 g in TB3 — training days and rest days alike | TB1 and TB3\n| Multivitamin\nthe books disagree about this one | one a day while building mass; TB1 takes one every couple of days as insurance | TB3\nLevel 2\n\n| Supplement | Dose | Source\n| Magnesium or magnesium/potassium\nhighly recommended for the active | 200 to 300 mg a day; glycinate or malate, or a combined magnesium/potassium | TB1, TB2 and TB3\n| Caffeine\none of the two things that made the most difference in TB1 | 100 to 200 mg, 20 to 30 minutes before training | TB1 and TB3\n| Citrulline malate\nworth having, not necessary | 4 to 6 g, 30 to 45 minutes before training | TB3\nNamed, but not endorsed\n\n| Supplement | Dose | Source\n| Zinc or ZMA\ncontroversial, and the book says so | as directed; ZMA is zinc, magnesium and vitamin B6 together | TB1\n| Vitamin D\nthe author is skeptical | about 1000 to 2000 every other day, and get your level tested first | TB1\n| B complex\noccasionally, not daily | once every two or three days, or after a hard session | TB1\n| Fish oil or EFAs\nthe author is skeptical | as directed — avoid mega-dosing | TB1\n| Beets or TMG\nworth having, not necessary | a couple of glasses of blended beets a day; TMG (betaine) if you would rather not | TB2\n| Electrolytes\nworth having, not necessary | magnesium, potassium and sodium, around long sessions | TB2\n\nA level-1 entry from TB III is level 1 for building mass, which is the book's own framing — creatine is not mandatory for somebody running Operator. The bottom table is the ones the books mention and then decline to recommend; they are listed because leaving them out would look like an oversight rather than a verdict.\n\n## Deloads and retests\n\nWhat the book does. Operator runs six-week blocks back to back and retests after two of them — twelve weeks, which it calls the optimal length of a strength phase. Six weeks is the minimum between tests and suits experienced lifters; waiting longer is fine, and if the loads still feel heavy the advice is to keep your current numbers rather than test on schedule. There is no deload week: the recovery it prescribes is a full week or more off every three to six months. Rest two to three days before a test day, ramp up, and take a 3–5 rep max rather than a true single if you prefer — the calculator does the rest.\n\nWhat we add. An optional scheduled deload, off by default, because a light week every few cycles suits running this year-round outside a unit. Turn it on in Setup if you want it.\n\nDeload weeks drop to 2×5 @ 60% (deadlift 1 set), swap all HIC to LISS, halve plyos and drop accessories. Retest weeks take three easy days, then heavy singles split across Thursday (squat, bench, jumps) and Saturday (deadlift, Lift 3, max pull-ups). Saturday's card feeds results into the next cycle.\nNeed a deload sooner? Add one from the Plan tab, or from the warning on Today after repeated hard sessions.\n\n## Calendar feed\n\nYour plan, subscribed to in the calendar app you already use. Every training day turns up as an all-day entry with the session on it: which lifts, the sets and reps, the percentage and the actual weight to put on the bar. Rest days are left out. It covers eighteen weeks ahead and a fortnight behind, and it is read-only, so nothing you do in your calendar changes your training.\n\nTurning it on. Setup → Calendar feed → Turn on the feed. You get a private web address ending in .ics. That address is all anyone needs to read your schedule, so treat it like a password: don’t post it anywhere, and don’t let it into a shared document.\n\nOn iPhone or iPad. The quickest way is the Add to iPhone button on that card, which opens Calendar with the address already filled in. Tap Subscribe, then Add. If the button does nothing, copy the address and go to Settings → Apps → Calendar → Calendar Accounts → Add Account → Other → Add Subscribed Calendar, and paste it there. On older versions of iOS the same screen is at Settings → Calendar → Accounts.\n\nOn Android. Google Calendar can only add a subscription on a computer, not in the phone app. Open calendar.google.com, find Other calendars in the left column, press +, choose From URL, paste the address and press Add calendar. Then open Google Calendar on the phone, go to the menu → Settings, find the new calendar in the list and turn Sync on. That last step catches people out: until you do it the calendar exists on your account but never appears on the phone.\n\nPutting it at the time you train. By default every session is an all-day entry, which sits at the top of the day and blocks nothing out. On the same Setup card, switch In the calendar to At a set time and give it your usual training time, and each session is booked properly instead: 75 minutes for a lifting day, 90 for a retest, and for conditioning however long the timer says that format actually runs, so a 23-minute MAP session books 23 minutes. If you train later at weekends, put that time in the Saturdays and Sundays box; leave it empty to use the same time all week.\n\nThe time is read as the local time wherever you are. Set 6am and it stays 6am in another country, rather than sliding to 1am because of the time difference. That is what you want for a training time, and it is why the entries carry no timezone.\n\nReminders. Pick one under Reminder and every session gets an alert: at the start, or 15 minutes, half an hour, one hour or two hours before. The reminder hangs off the session time, so it needs At a set time to mean anything precise. If you would rather keep all-day entries, the one reminder on offer there is 8pm the night before, which is the useful one anyway for packing a bag. Reminders are off until you choose one.\n\nHow often it updates. The app writes the calendar, not the server, because the schedule is worked out on your phone and a second copy on the server would eventually disagree with it. So the feed is as fresh as the last time you opened the app. Move a week, change a max, swap a lift: open the app once and the calendar follows. Your calendar app then picks the change up on its own schedule. iPhone usually does that within a few hours, and you can force it by pulling down in Calendar. Google is slow and can take most of a day for the first sync and for later changes. That is Google’s refresh interval, not something this app can hurry.\n\nWhat is in it, and what is not. The feed carries the schedule only: dates, session types, lifts, prescriptions and working weights. It does not carry what you logged, your maxes, bodyweight, measurements, readiness scores or anything about your account. Someone who found the address would learn what you are planning to lift, and nothing else.\n\nIf you need to revoke it. New address on the same card issues a fresh one and stops the old address working immediately. Use it if the link gets out. You then have to re-subscribe on your devices, because the old subscription is pointing at an address that no longer exists. Turn off removes the feed entirely.\n\nIf the calendar says it cannot connect. Check the address is the current one, since New address invalidates the previous link. If it is right and it still fails, open this app once to make sure the feed has been written, then try again.\n\n## Warm-up\n\n1 · Raise temp Bike, rower or easy jog, 4–5 min\n2 · Breathe 90/90 breathing ×5 breaths · cat/cow ×8–10\n3 · T-spine Thread the needle 6–8/side · open book 8/side · quadruped extension 8/side\n4 · Hips 90/90 switches ×10 · 90/90 lean 20–30 s/side · couch stretch 45–60 s/side · frog rocks ×10 · world's greatest ×5/side\n5 · Activate Leg swings 10 each way · band pull-aparts + dislocates ×15 · glute bridge or BW squat ×10\nShort version (7 min): bike 4 min, 90/90 breathing, cat/cow, 90/90 switches, couch stretch, band pull-aparts. Keep static holds easy before heavy squats.\n\nAnything with a time on it has a ⏱ next to it, on the day's card and inside session mode. Tap it and the timer runs; per-side holds count both sides. “Guide me through it” is the hands-free version of the same list, not the only way to get a clock.\n\nSession mode shows one ramp set at a time with − and + beside it, stepping by that lift's rounding increment, because the bar does not come in every weight a percentage asks for. What you set is written to that row, the same place typing it on the day's card writes to, so the plates and the “Next” line follow it.\n\nBetween ramp sets the rest is 90 seconds by default, set per lift in the warm-up block on each lift card. Anything under a minute is no longer on offer — it is not long enough once the bar is heavy. If you want more on the day, the running timer takes +30 s as many times as you like.\n\n## Warm-up and mobility library\nEvery movement in the warm-up and the mobility blocks, with what it is for and the ways it usually goes wrong. The same entries open from the checklists on the day.\n\nRaise and reset\n\n### Raise the temperature\n\nSetup. Any steady machine or an easy jog. Nothing that needs a decision.\nExecution. Four to five minutes at a pace you could hold a conversation at, building slightly. You want warm, not tired.\nCues. Finish breathing a little deeper and faintly warm. If you are sweating hard you have gone too far.\nCommon errors. Treating it as conditioning. Skipping it because the gym is already warm — the point is blood flow and joint fluid, not air temperature.\n\n### 90/90 breathing\n\nSetup. On your back, feet flat on a bench or against a wall, hips and knees at 90 degrees. Low back flat.\nExecution. Breathe in through the nose for four, out through the mouth for six to eight, emptying completely. Five breaths.\nCues. The ribs should move down and in on the exhale, not up. Push gently through the heels to keep the pelvis tucked.\nCommon errors. Arching the low back on the inhale. Rushing the exhale — the long out-breath is what settles the nervous system before a heavy session.\nThirty seconds, and it is the difference between walking in distracted and starting the first warm-up set present.\n\n### Cat / cow\n\nSetup. On hands and knees, hands under shoulders, knees under hips.\nExecution. Round the spine one segment at a time from the tailbone up, then reverse into extension the same way. Slow, eight to ten reps.\nCues. Move the whole spine, not just the lower back. Breathe out as you round, in as you extend.\nCommon errors. Hinging at one point and letting the rest stay rigid. Going fast enough that it becomes a rock rather than a stretch.\n\n### Legs up the wall breathing\n\nSetup. On your back with the legs resting up a wall, backside close to it.\nExecution. Two to three minutes of slow nasal breathing, long exhales.\nCues. Nothing to achieve. Let the legs be heavy and make the out-breath longer than the in-breath.\nCommon errors. Checking your phone. Treating it as a stretch and pushing the hamstrings.\nThe best-value two minutes on an off day: it is the one block that is purely about coming down.\n\nSpine and t-spine\n\n### Thread the needle\n\nSetup. On hands and knees. One arm reaches up toward the ceiling, eyes following the hand.\nExecution. Thread that arm under the opposite shoulder, reaching across until the shoulder and ear rest toward the floor, then open back up. Six to eight per side.\nCues. Rotate from the ribs, not the neck. Keep the hips square and over the knees the whole time.\nCommon errors. Letting the hips swing away to get more range. Twisting the head instead of the thoracic spine.\n\n### Open book\n\nSetup. On your side, knees stacked and bent at 90 degrees, arms straight out in front, palms together. A foam roller or cushion under the top knee keeps the hips honest.\nExecution. Keeping the knees pinned, open the top arm in a wide arc toward the floor behind you and follow it with your eyes. Hold a breath at the end, then return. Eight per side.\nCues. The knees staying down is the exercise. Exhale into the last few degrees rather than forcing it.\nCommon errors. Letting the top knee lift, which turns it into a lumbar twist. Snapping back rather than controlling the return.\n\n### Quadruped t-spine extension\n\nSetup. On hands and knees, one hand behind the head, elbow out.\nExecution. Rotate the elbow down toward the opposite wrist, then open it up toward the ceiling, opening the chest. Eight per side.\nCues. Sit back toward the heels a little first — it locks the low back out and makes the mid-back do the work.\nCommon errors. Arching the lower back to fake the range. Letting the supporting elbow collapse.\n\n### Thoracic extension over a foam roller\n\nSetup. Roller across the mid-back, hands behind the head, hips down or lifted.\nExecution. Extend back over the roller, breathe out at the end, come up, move the roller an inch and repeat. Ten slow reps up the mid-back.\nCues. Stay above the bottom of the ribs — never extend over the low back. The exhale is what gets the range.\nCommon errors. Rolling up and down fast. Flaring the ribs and arching the lumbar spine instead of extending the thoracic one.\n\n### Child’s pose with lat reach\n\nSetup. Kneeling, sitting back toward the heels, arms extended in front.\nExecution. Walk both hands to one side until you feel it down the opposite side of the ribs and under the armpit. Hold 45 seconds per side.\nCues. Keep the hips back over the heels and press the opposite ribs toward the floor.\nCommon errors. Letting the hips drift forward, which turns it into a shoulder stretch instead of a lat one.\n\n### Dead hang\n\nSetup. A bar you can hang from with the feet clear, or knees bent if it is low.\nExecution. Hang relaxed for 20 to 30 seconds, letting the shoulders come up around the ears and the spine lengthen.\nCues. Let it be passive. Breathe. Grip is the usual limit, not the shoulders.\nCommon errors. Holding an active shrug-down the whole time — that is a different exercise. Dropping off rather than stepping down.\nThe cheapest spinal decompression there is, and it doubles as grip work on a deadlift day.\n\nHips\n\n### 90/90 hip switches\n\nSetup. Sitting on the floor, one leg bent in front at 90 degrees, the other out to the side at 90. Hands behind you at first.\nExecution. Keeping the feet where they are, drop both knees across to the other side and sit up tall. Ten switches.\nCues. Lead with the knees, not the torso. Chest stays tall — hands on the floor behind you is fine while the hips are tight.\nCommon errors. Rolling the pelvis back and rounding the spine to get down. Dropping the knees with a bang rather than lowering them.\n\n### 90/90 lean-forward hold\n\nSetup. In the 90/90 position, front shin roughly parallel to your chest.\nExecution. Sit tall, then hinge forward over the front shin and hold for 20 to 45 seconds, breathing.\nCues. Hinge at the hip with a long spine. The stretch should be in the outside of the front hip, not the knee.\nCommon errors. Rounding over instead of hinging. Any pain on the inside of the front knee — open the angle until there is none.\n\n### Couch stretch\n\nSetup. Back foot up on a bench, sofa or against a wall, knee on a pad close to the base. Front foot planted.\nExecution. Squeeze the back glute and tuck the pelvis under, then come up tall. Hold 60 to 90 seconds per side, breathing.\nCues. The pelvic tuck is the whole exercise. If you can lean back comfortably straight away, you have not tucked.\nCommon errors. Arching the low back to get upright, which feels like more stretch and is less. Knee pain from being too far from the wall or on a hard floor.\nThree lifting days plus riding is a lot of hip flexor in a shortened position. This is the single most valuable thing in the mobility list.\n\n### Frog stretch\n\nSetup. Knees wide on a padded floor, shins in line with the knees, ankles flexed, forearms down.\nExecution. Rock the hips gently back toward the heels and forward again, ten slow rocks, going a little further as it opens.\nCues. Keep the low back neutral and let the movement come from the hips. Rock, do not sit and strain.\nCommon errors. Pushing into a static end-range hold on cold adductors. Letting the knees slide wider than is comfortable.\n\n### World’s greatest stretch\n\nSetup. From a long lunge, front foot flat, back leg straight, hands on the floor inside the front foot.\nExecution. Drop the back of the front hip toward the floor, then rotate the inside arm up to the ceiling and follow it with your eyes. Five per side.\nCues. Back knee off the floor and back leg long. The rotation comes from the mid-back with the hips stable.\nCommon errors. Letting the back knee drop, which removes the hip flexor part. Rotating with a bent back leg and calling it done.\n\n### Figure-4 glute stretch\n\nSetup. On your back, one ankle crossed over the opposite thigh just above the knee.\nExecution. Reach through and pull the supporting thigh toward you until you feel it in the crossed glute. Hold 45 seconds per side.\nCues. Keep the crossed knee pushed gently away. Head and shoulders stay down.\nCommon errors. Pulling on the crossed knee itself. Lifting the head and neck to get closer.\n\n### Pigeon\n\nSetup. Front shin across in front of you, back leg long behind. A cushion under the front hip if it does not reach the floor.\nExecution. Square the hips forward and fold over the front shin. Hold 60 to 90 seconds per side.\nCues. Hips square is what makes it work. Back toes pointed straight back, not out to the side.\nCommon errors. Collapsing onto the front hip, which twists the knee. Ignoring front-knee pain — use figure-4 on your back instead, it does the same job.\n\n### Standing quad stretch\n\nSetup. Hold something for balance, heel pulled to the backside.\nExecution. Tuck the pelvis, then draw the knee back and down. Hold 45 seconds per side.\nCues. Squeeze the glute on the side you are stretching. Knees stay together.\nCommon errors. Arching the back and calling it a stretch. Yanking the ankle into the backside, which is a knee complaint waiting to happen.\n\nHamstrings and posterior chain\n\n### Standing forward fold\n\nSetup. Feet hip width, knees soft, not locked.\nExecution. Hinge and let the upper body hang, hands wherever they reach. Hold 45 to 60 seconds, breathing out into it.\nCues. Soft knees and a long spine beat straight legs and a rounded back. Let gravity do it.\nCommon errors. Locking the knees and bouncing. Chasing the floor with the fingers at the cost of the hamstrings doing the work.\n\n### Seated forward fold\n\nSetup. Sitting with legs straight out, sit bones under you. Sit on a folded towel if the low back rounds immediately.\nExecution. Hinge forward with a long spine, reaching for the feet, and hold 45 to 60 seconds.\nCues. Lead with the chest, not the head. The last thing to round is the lower back.\nCommon errors. Rounding from the start and pulling on the toes. Bouncing.\n\n### Supine hamstring stretch\n\nSetup. On your back, a strap, belt or towel around the foot of one leg. A doorway works instead.\nExecution. Raise the leg with the knee nearly straight until you feel the hamstring, keeping the other leg flat. Hold 45 seconds per side.\nCues. Keep the opposite leg on the floor and the pelvis down. A slight knee bend is fine.\nCommon errors. Letting the other leg creep up, which hides the range. Pulling into pain behind the knee rather than mid-belly stretch.\n\nAnkles and feet\n\n### Straight-leg calf stretch\n\nSetup. Hands on a wall, one leg back, heel down, both feet pointing forward.\nExecution. Keep the back knee straight and press the hips forward until the upper calf stretches. Hold 45 seconds per side.\nCues. Back heel stays glued down and the back foot points straight ahead, not out.\nCommon errors. Letting the heel lift, which removes the stretch. Turning the foot out to fake range.\n\n### Bent-knee calf stretch\n\nSetup. Same position as the straight-leg version.\nExecution. Bend the back knee while keeping the heel down. The stretch drops lower, near the Achilles. Hold 45 seconds per side.\nCues. A small bend is enough. This one targets the soleus, which is what a plyo session beats up.\nCommon errors. Bending so far the heel lifts. Skipping it because the straight-leg version felt like enough — they are different muscles.\n\n### Knee-to-wall ankle mobilization\n\nSetup. Stand facing a wall, toes a few inches back from it, foot pointing straight at it.\nExecution. Drive the knee forward over the middle of the foot until it touches the wall, keeping the heel down. Ten reps per side, moving the foot back as it improves.\nCues. Heel stays down and the knee tracks over the second toe, not inward.\nCommon errors. Letting the heel lift or the arch collapse. Measuring progress by distance alone while losing the heel.\nAnkle range is the quietest limiter on squat depth and the most common reason a landing collapses.\n\n### Foot and arch roll\n\nSetup. A lacrosse or tennis ball under the arch of a bare foot, seated or standing.\nExecution. Roll slowly heel to toe for 60 seconds per side, pausing a few seconds on sore spots.\nCues. Slow enough to feel individual spots. Pressure you can breathe through, not pressure you brace against.\nCommon errors. Rolling fast, which does nothing. Standing full weight on a hard ball on a sore foot.\n\nActivation\n\n### Leg swings\n\nSetup. Hold something solid. Stand tall on one leg.\nExecution. Swing the free leg front to back ten times, then side to side ten times, building range gradually. Both legs.\nCues. Stay upright and let the leg swing from a relaxed hip. Range builds over the set — the first swing is not the biggest.\nCommon errors. Throwing the leg to its end range on rep one. Twisting the whole torso to get height.\n\n### Band pull-aparts and dislocates\n\nSetup. A light band held at shoulder width for pull-aparts, and wide for dislocates. Wide enough that nothing pinches.\nExecution. Fifteen pull-aparts, squeezing the shoulder blades together with straight arms, then fifteen slow dislocates taking the band overhead and behind.\nCues. Keep the ribs down and the neck long. Widen the grip on dislocates until the path is smooth.\nCommon errors. Shrugging on the pull-aparts. A band so heavy the dislocates turn into a shoulder jam — lighter and wider is better.\n\n### Glute bridge or bodyweight squat\n\nSetup. On your back, feet flat and close to the hips — or standing, for squats.\nExecution. Ten reps. Bridges: drive through the heels and squeeze the glutes at the top. Squats: full depth, controlled.\nCues. Finish the bridge with the glutes, not the low back. The squats are a rehearsal of your pattern, not a set.\nCommon errors. Hyperextending the lower back at the top of the bridge. Rushing — this is the last thing before the bar, so it should look like what you are about to do.\n\nAgile 8 · Joe DeFranco\n\n### Foam roll IT band\n\nSetup. On your side on the roller, the outer thigh on it, top leg forward for balance and weight on the forearm.\nExecution. Start just under the hip and roll up and down to mid-thigh ten to fifteen times. Then roll from mid-thigh down to the outside of the knee, another ten to fifteen. Work the tight spots.\nCues. Slow. The roller should move about an inch a second, not sweep the whole leg.\nCommon errors. Rolling fast enough that nothing changes. Rolling straight over the knee or the hip bone rather than stopping short of both.\n\n### Foam roll adductors\n\nSetup. Face down with the roller under the inside of one thigh, that leg out to the side at about ninety degrees.\nExecution. Same method as the IT band, on the inner thigh. Start just below the groin crease, roll up and down to mid-thigh ten to fifteen times, then from mid-thigh to the inside of the knee for another ten to fifteen.\nCues. Let the leg be heavy. Shift your weight onto the roller rather than pressing with the arms.\nCommon errors. Staying too far from the groin, which is usually where the tight part is.\n\n### Glute and piriformis release, tennis ball\n\nSetup. Sitting on the ball on one cheek, tilted slightly onto that side, with that leg crossed over the other knee.\nExecution. Roll around for about thirty seconds, then switch sides.\nCues. Crossing the leg is what opens the glute up to the ball. Hunt for the spot rather than rolling evenly.\nCommon errors. Sitting square, which puts the ball on bone rather than muscle.\nDeFranco’s whole note on this one: “Feel free to cry.”\n\n### Rollovers into V-sits\n\nSetup. Sitting on the floor, knees tucked, hands on the floor behind you.\nExecution. Roll backwards onto your shoulders and take the knees to the ears, then roll forward into a straddle and reach for the toes. Ten reps, continuous.\nCues. Use the momentum of the roll to get into the straddle rather than hauling yourself up.\nCommon errors. Rolling onto the neck instead of the upper back. Going so fast the forward reach never happens.\nDeFranco shows this one in a video rather than describing it, so this is the standard movement, not his wording.\n\n### Fire hydrant circles\n\nSetup. On hands and knees, back flat, weight even.\nExecution. Lift one knee out to the side and draw a circle with it, ten forward and ten backward, then the other leg.\nCues. Make the circle at the hip. The knee stays bent throughout.\nCommon errors. Rotating the whole torso to get a bigger circle. Letting the low back arch.\nDeFranco shows this one in a video rather than describing it, so this is the standard movement, not his wording.\n\n### Mountain climbers\n\nSetup. A push-up position, hands under the shoulders, body in a line.\nExecution. Drive one knee to the chest and switch feet, twenty in total. Brisk, not a sprint — this is a warm-up.\nCues. Hips stay low and level. The shoulders should not travel back over the hands.\nCommon errors. Letting the hips ride up. Treating it as conditioning and arriving at the bar out of breath.\nDeFranco shows this one in a video rather than describing it, so this is the standard movement, not his wording.\n\n### Groiners\n\nSetup. A push-up position.\nExecution. Jump both feet up outside the hands, then back. Ten reps, and hold the last one for ten seconds.\nCues. In the hold, use your upper arms to push the knees outward while you drop your butt down. That is DeFranco’s own cue.\nCommon errors. Landing with the feet behind the hands, which gives you none of the hip opening.\n\n### Static hip flexor stretch\n\nSetup. Half-kneeling, back knee on a pad, front foot flat, and tuck the pelvis under before you start.\nExecution. Three holds of ten seconds on one leg, then all three on the other. Finish one side before switching.\nCues. The stretch comes from the pelvic tuck and squeezing the back glute, not from pushing the hips forward.\nCommon errors. Arching the low back to feel more. Alternating sides instead of doing three in a row, which is not what the routine asks for.";
